      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>GREAT SANKEY.</head>
        <p>GREAT SANKEY.</p>
        <p>THE BURIAL GROUND.—On Friday afternoon the I Lord Bishop of Liverpool (Dr. Chavasse) performed , the ceremony of consecrating another portion of the 1 burial ground at Great Sankey. The land consecrated I lies on the east side of the-ehapel and the site contains 5,010 square yards, and will allow for no fewer than 763 interments. The first consecration took place on the 20th June, 1881, when the ceremony was performed by the late Bishop of the Diocese (Dr. Ryle). Altogether about five acres were set apart as a burial ground, and a large quantity of the land has yet to be consecrated, although the s4e consecrated on Friday afternoon will supply the needs of the parish so far as I burial accommodation is concerned for many years.—The service was very largely attended. —At half-past three the Bishop and the clergy met in the chapel, where they robed. after which a procession I was formed and proceeded rptind the ground, the Bishop, and clergy repeating several Psalms. The Registrar of the diocese, who wore a wig and gown, walked first, followed by the Bishop. Then came the Vicar of the Parish (the Rev. J. R. Jones), the Rev. J. H. G. Honing- worth), who carried the Bishop's stall; the Rev. H. Gibson Smith (Rural Dean of Childwall), Mr. H. Fair- dough (vicar's warden), Mr. William Woods (people's warden), the Rev. John Gunnell and Cecil Parker (Wesleyan ministers stationed at Penketh), Mr. William Taylor (deputy chairman of the Great Sankey Parish Council), and Mr. Joseph Smith (clerk), Dr. Murray (chairman of the Penketh Parish Council), and the following members :—Messrs. W. Hodgkinson, R, Rout- ledge, J. Ditchfield, David Houghton, J. Ashcroft, and E. Foster (clerk).—On the return to a temporary plat- form which had been provided near the chapel, the sentence of consecration of the new burial ground was read by the Registrar, after which the Bishop offered several prayers. Then the hymn, commencing " Brief life is here our portion," was sung and the formal ceremony of consecration came to an end.—The bishop having delivered a short address, an adjournment was made to the schoolroom where his Lordship held a reception. In the evening a service was held in the Church, the Bishop preaching.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>POSTMAN'S RHEUMATISM. Every Step Exciting Pain, Tortured Night and Day, Worn Away to a Skeleton. Found New and Good flood in</head>
        <p>A POSTMAN'S RHEUMATISM. Every Step Exciting Pain, Tortured Night and Day, Worn Away to a Skeleton. Found New and Good flood in</p>
        <p>D. Williams' Pirl% Elisa</p>
        <p>"When people saw me hobbling about so painfully, cribbled by Rheumatism,l bad sympathy from all sides," said Mr. Geo. C. Wicks, 54, Enbrook-street, Harrow- road, London, W., for several years a postman in the district. Rheumatism reduced him to a hopeless, tottering wreck until Dr. Williams' Pink Pills drove out the poisons of Rheumatism and made him a healthy active man again. "I was worried one night," he said, " with a sharp pain in my right foot that kept me awake. In the morninn. I could not get my boot on. The pains travelled up my leg to my knee, and I could only totter about with the aid of sticks. I attended St. Mary's Hospital for months. A surgeon carefully examined me, and said my complete recovery was doubtful, as I had chronic rheumatism. I felt terribly dejected. It looked RS if I was to be a burden to my people all my life. "I hardly ate enough to keep myself al:ve. My face , was drawn with pain, and I was nervous, irritable, and easily upset by noises. For, three weeks I was too weak -</p>
        <p>to sit up in bed, and was Mn. worn away to a skeleton. My legs, from my knees (From a photograph.) down, were practically useless. If I tried to put my foot to the ground, sharp pains would shoot up my legs and cause me to writhe in agony. No treatment gave relief. "Then I made up my mind to take a proper course of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills. I bought some, and soon began to feel a change for the better. Gradnally I became stronger, and the pains ceased. I made rapid progress, anebefore long all traces of Rheumatism had left me. I could walk again with anyone; my appetite and spirits were good;Dr. Williams, Pink Pills literally put me on my feet again, to the astonishment of my friends."</p>
        <p>Dr. Williams Pink Pills cure, by making Rich, Red Blood and feeding the Nerves. In this way they have cured Anmmia, Indigestion, Eczema, Rheumatism, Sciatica, St. Vitus' Dance, Neuralgia, Paralysis, and the special trouble of girls and women. 2s. 9d, for one box, or 13s. Od. for six, post free, from Dr. Williams' Medicine Co., Holborn-viaduct, London; sold also by dealers, but mind you ask for Dr. Williams', for substi- tutes never cure.</p>
