itting hostess , for these entertainments could be found than Lady ! I a frontier war to aid her hero by sending a cipher Colehrooke, who is a daughter of the late Lord Alfred I ; cablegram in Sir Daniel's name requesting the Paget. She is a godchild of King Edward and Queen ; 1 Commander-in-Chief in India to provide Blundell Alexandra, and both their Majesties were present at her : with employment in a distinguished position. wedding. Whilst the Lord High Commissioner remains! the representative of the King, a state of semi-royalty ; is kept up at ancient Holyrood, and the banquets and I receptions are regarded as the concluding events of the ' Edinburgh season. l Threatening the Bachelors.—The unmarried man has not been mach disturbed by the perfunctory proposal I to tax bachelors, recently put forward in the House of I Commons, but it is generally thought that when women I have the Parliamentary vote, this question will assume I a more serious import to bachelordom. It is assumed ; that if baehelordom were made onerous, men would be i more ready to marry, and women be more in request, I not to say a more marketable commodity. Some dis- tinction would however have to be made between I bachelors who can afford to marry, and those who can- I not. Another difficulty after having decided to tax I him, would be how to catch him, as the bachelor,having no one to toil for, except himself is ratber an elusive I creature—here to-day, and gone tomorrow. Then again . any such tax must assume that every man could get i married if he would, a proposition more than doubtful 1 when there are so many bachelor girls who prefer to traverse their oivn free and easy way, through life.! Altogether the bachelors seem pretty safe, and it is ' doubtful if they would have anything to fear even if women had the Parliamentary franchise. A Disadvantage of the Motor.—The craze for light ' coloured boots and shoes for our door wear in London and all our large cities and towns is likely to beco