        <p>COMMERCIAL FAILURES.—AtCOIding to " Kemp's Mercantile Gazette," the total number of Commercial Failures recorded in England and Wales during the week ending Friday, May 25th, was 175, viz. :—New Bankruptcy Proceedings published in the London Gazette, 93; Deeds of Arrangement registered, 82. The respective numbers in the corresponding week of last year were : Bankruptcies, 104 ; Deeds of Arrangement 92—total 190 ; being an increase of 21. The totul num- ber of Corarnercit.l Failures recorded during the 21 weeks of the present year is 3,544 ; the total number recorded in thp corresponding 21 weeks of last year was 3,791, showing a decrease of 217.</p>
        <p>"But there,</p>
        <p>I Sir Dan was lying on the, sofa whi.a, Madge, + ,thatted under cover of- Annette's playing. Ha smiled grimly. "I think I could puzzle' you, ray dear." Madge turned up her hose and looked at him over it. "I defy you. If it's a cipher, that is, and not a code." "Oh, I'll be fair, Madge," said Sir Dan, and setting off the sofa- he opened the door into his own sitting-room. Madge jumped up with beating heart and followeil him to the door. ,"Come in," said the Adjutant-General, blandly. Sitting at his desk he unlocked a drawer and took .ou.: a little card. "But don't come too near," said Sir Dan. "Of course, if you saw how I do it you [could make it out easily." And in ten minutes he gave her a short cipher of fourteen letters. "Discover hat " "Is it a figure 'cipher ? " asked Madge, brightly. Sir Dan laughed. "Well, I don't mind saying it is not." He locked up his drawer and went 'back to the drawing-room. ",I shall never hear about it again, _Madge." "You will." "If it heats you ? " "Of course," cried Madge. And she meant it. For she had little hope 1 indeed of discovering it; and she did not dare ask J him yet if was the one he used himself when he telegraphed to "Chief, Simla." But having got it, what was she to do? Nothing yet, she declared. She knew where Sir Dan kept his cipher-key when he was at home, and she meant to get at it and copy it. If it was wicked she couldn't help it. Richard should not stay at Duni-Dum. To k6ep him there was wicked; to get I him away was a virtuous act. Yet to get at that drawer meant false keys, or it meant stealing keys. At the very least it would be a dishonourable act—a petty, mean act; or it would have meant that but for the end she had in view. The end made all the difference. Richard's good was the end. Dear, dear Richard! Very likely he was thinking of her now, perhaps wonder- ing if he would ever see her again. Oh, oh But even if he never, never saw her she would.have done something for him. She was the little mouse, he the black lion in the red tape net: She cried for an hour, and then cheered' up and bathed her eyes and dabbed them dry, and felt much more virtuous foa having had a miserable time of tears and suffering. So while Billy was engaged in fixing up the last of his outfit, and in buying a huge mass of entirely unnecessary articles, she haunted Annette, and was with hei every hour. And each morning during a week she tried Sir Dan's drawer, always finding it locked. For she went in early, just when Annette was in her bath and Sir Dan was doing his con- stitutional in the park. None of , her keys fitted; her hand shook, and she was in a fever of appre- hension. If she were caught! And on the fifth day she found the drawer unlocked. Inside was the cipher written plainly on a card, the card Sir Dan had used when he gave her that little bit of hieroglyphic. She read the key-word, and ran back into the drawing-room panting. She sat on the sofa and stared at herself in a long glass opposite, and wondered how it was she had been born so wicked. But when her heart-beats grew less flurried, she was glad. Now she could do what she wanted. Not for long would Richard have to shoot snipe at Dum-Dum ; his "dear little girl" would soon put an end to that. But her heart stopped beating. How did she know whether this particular cipher was one ireally understood by the people at Simla? She did not know at all. But she would know. Having gone so far she might just as well go the rest of the way. And, after all, Sir Dan was a horrid old wretch, and deserved being cheated. • She stayed for breakfast. "Oh, Sir Dan, you said you would never hear again of that cipher if I couldn't make it out. "I did," replied Sir Dan, who was engaged in active operations against the enemy, and could scarcely speak. He always went through breakfast as if it was a campaign, and had a way of swoop- ing down on any aikitcle of diet as if he had taken it by surprise. "Then you told a'story," said Madge, for it haarbeaten me, and I own it. . "Very honourable of you, I'm sure," cried Sir Dan, assaulting a pie entrenched in a stiff sangar of pastry. "Very honourable." Madge flinched. "Oh, not at all. But do tell me what it means." The Adjutant-General, having demolished the pie, was turning his attention to the dry toast. "Can't, my dear. It's official, or partly so." And catching sight of some jam lying in ambuscade behind a Black Mountain of wholemeal bread, he cleaned it up in the most businesslike manner. "Oh, if it's official, of course I oughtn't to ask Ton," cried Madge, hurriedly. "But how ao they had it out in India? They have the key?" "To be sure," said the Adjutant-General, as he congratulated himself in a general order on the satisfactory results of breakfast. "And now I do know," cried Madge to herself. "So it's all right." She asked Sir Dan another question. "Is there going to be a row this time on ths frontier? " "Can't tell, I'm sure," said Sir Dan, and. imitating another great person, he added: "I haven't read the papela this morning."</p>
        <p>CHAPTER IX. "That was human nature, of course. Detestable human nature," cried Madge. "For if it was not detestable Sir Dan would never have been so horrible, or, to say the least, would have repented by now." Then there was the wording of the telegram. Would she not give it away as unofficial by its very phrasing. She tried a hundred forms, and read every military book she could get to imbue herself with style, the proper style. Perhaps if it was unofficial and. from Sir Dan as Sir Dan, and not quite as the Adjutant-General, it need not be so neat and tight. She wrote a few: 1 1. "Please send Major Blundell, now at Dum- Dum, to the front." ' 2. "Please send Major Blundell, now at Dom- ; Dom, with North-West Frontier Expedition." ; 3. "Give Major Blundell, Dum-Dum, employ- ment at Gilgit." . 1 4. "Commander-in-Chief asks that Major !Blundell may be employed with forth(oming • expedition." . ; And so on a hundred times. But they looked ; , bald and curt, or unofficial and unconvincing. 1. "Chief is instructed to request that Major ;Blundell (now at Dum-Duni), may be employed on t frontier." 1 ' She hit on one at last which pleased her more. For "instructed to request he had seen or I heard somewhere, and though it was vague it lobviously meant the highest influence, for who but Lord Pytchley could instruct the Commander-in- Chief? They might even think that H.R.H. the ; Duke of Oxford had come down to ask that it ; should be done. That would be a joke. i She remembered Richard's remark about baggage , , camels, and amended her work. I "Chief is instructed to request urgently that ; Major Blundell, Punjab Cavalry, be employed on , Statl or any special important duty with expedition." i There would be no baggage camels for him if athat went. He might go up as interpreter or as et special political.. v She signed this "Fielding," and addressed it "Chief, Simla." Then she turned it into an easy cipher, and thought it looked more mysterious ana, important. , It took her nearly two months and twenty talks with General This and Colonel That to get so far. The old soldiers thought she was a duck and .a darling, and marvelled at her interest in a Service which was obviously going to the devil. And in India, which could net long remain a possession of the British Crown, first because we had never taken Afghanistan once for all, sir, and secondly, because we had overridden Lord Laurence's policy and had been fools enough, idiots enough to go beyond the Punjab, -Madge, where, entrenched and at ease, we could over- whelm the Russians as they came in one by one asking for a drink by the Khyber, and the Bolan,_ ' notoriously very thirsty places. Exactly' so, said Madge i tha't was what she always thought. But about this telegram in her little story ?—which she mustn't mention on any account. And all the time she was concocting her plot she had her Own little wars with her mother, who 'treated her as a refractory hill-tribe, and organised expeditions of chastisement, sometimesapenetrating to the remote fastness of her bed-chamber, and sometimes being repelled with great slaughter 0/3 the stairs or in the passes of the passages. It was only when she . drew Madge down on the plains of the drawing-room or dining-reom that she got the best of her, for there Madge had to observe the rules of. social, civilised warfare, and the Geneva conventions of polite quarrel. And she could not cut her 'nether up in detail when Mrs. Gretton was in battle array with dowagers in reserve to be , , brought up when it seemed desirable. But the summer was gone at last, and then half the autumn, and Billy got the route. He was to ; a out with 6. draft of artillery, mid was greatly ; CHAPTER X. . go cock-a-hoop, considering himself a 'very desirable ; is highly probable that Sir Dan did not Especially as he was to go to a mountain battery. ' i require the papers to know whether there was going to be trouble somewhere on the borders of the : It . addition to her Majesty's forces in India. He came down to Gretton House for three days, 1 Punjab or in that, blessed country lying both sides nd Gilgif. For and said good-bye to the dogs and horses and ; human retainers of the family, and if •he shed a! that 1 of the Indus between Rawtil Pindi a tear over One old dog he was never likely to see where was obvious even to the limited intelligence again he Nvas none tlie worse for that. And it of a believer in an Indian nation. Solos of the Madge -shed a tear Over him she was certainly the ; "practical manceuvres" were at hand some- Enolisli newspapers, whose clients were chiefly better for it, because the self-centred life she led t traders, and therefore always averse to any war just then was doing her very little good. Yet halt I 1 which did not directly promote tile interests her tears were fox... Richard, who probably had once! of trade, already screamed for takind everything g ' swaggered round like Billy, and had been so cruelly 1 ivin e down. They dilated on the noble qualities -disappointed. &lt;-1 Swatis, Chitralis, Afridis, and Kaffirs, and pro- " Don't get hay of them down on you out there, tested by implication that if any Political got his Billy," she implored, throat cut it was notoriously part of his business "The very notion ! I say, Madge, why should to risk the continuity of his carotids, and I? " asked the aggrieved young gunner, probably his own fault if they were severed. The "Oh, I don't knew,- •Billy," sobbed Madge, "but noble savage could do no wrong, while, the British you m—might." ; soldier was a 'howling, , outraging, looting "You're a foolish girl," said Billy, scoundrel, to Whom any Masi, drunk .. with I won't." fanaticism, was a quiet and gentlemanlY Wesleyan. Madge cheered up. • All of which read well and was satisfactory; to "And if you meet any of my heroes, ;Billy, give Clapham. 1 them my love." , ~,! • But now it would have been highly satisfactory "Oh, certainly," said Billy, absently. "‘-'!' to Madge to hear of certain peace. She had no !course—oh, what was it you, said? Oh, no, a theught of anyone but Richard. For him India i won't. What rot, Madge." i existed, for hint the wild tribes objected to forts of I "Not even to Captain Melville and Captain observation, for him was an army corns getting !Blundell ?" ready quietly to settle such objectors. Witheut a I "Not to•Bliintlell," growled Billy. , war he could get no chance. With it, and with I "If you ever see him, you'll adore him," cried her behind him, he would help to extend the ; his sister. "lie's just the man you'd worship, I Empire, and would set the Indus on fire. I know you." i Having now got her gun ready and her telegram ! "You don't," said Billy, indignantly. "The put into cipher she had another difficulty which ; very idea. I say, come now. But I suppose I . , gte4 a.ly treinbled her. In the ordinary course of : must go in and see mother." , events she was well aware that every telegram and ; He saw Mrs. Greaten, nod she save him a great letter would be acknowledged by the first mail. If I deal of good advice, and elmered him up by hers was so acknowledged it would come back to ;suggesting, in serallchral toms, that long ere he the War Office, and, going to the Adjutant- i General's Department, might easily be detected as returned she would be at rest in the family tomb, , t partially owing to the decadence of moral tone in a forgery. It depended simply on an accident—Sir i might see the acknowledgment or he might the Courtly, and the intrusion of the brewing Da, -sister . not. She suspected that as a rule he would not go desired—is much, . over such papers, having more important work to _ being, scali.„Toe j. Aelreaenn..te,t,anwdhiephailtelfyt tomtnicieb.stcoondbieicdt and stitched down on to a band of velvet, eithei ; '=-- i - the crossover style, the froat- black or coloured, while a band of fine tucks I very nine'l. ' CiO. And yet, of course, her luck might be bad, further ornaments e