        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>DRESS-FASHION-HOME. •</p>
        <p>And Sir Dan went to Perlis for three days. When she found that out she ran straight to her sent at once to a mountain battery which was under father, orders for the front, and was going up with one "Take me over to Paris for a week, dad." of the relieving forces to Gulistan. -He felt three Mr. Gretton stared at his erratic child. ! inches taller; -his sublime head touched the stars. "My dear Madge--" , The whole station was in a ferment, for stores "Oh, do I feel like it," cried Madge. were pouring in and transport being collected on "Your mother 'expects us back to-moirow, every hand. The roads were blocked by mules and suggested Mr. Gretton, rather Weakly. groaning camels, with a motley crowd of black- " But you'd like to yourself, wouldn't you P Oh, guard drivers. Billy had little time to himself; do, dad. neither he nor any of the other freshly-joined "I shan't have any peace when I get back,' said subalterns had hny chance of paying calls, for every her father, more doubtfully, moment was precious now. The advance was really "If you don't, you won't," cried the audacious beginning at once. Colonel Briggs, who was in girl. "I'll smoke cigarettes again and let mother command of the column which was to tackle the smell them." higher passes, was not the man to waste an hour. The last time this had occurred was as important The transport and commissariat officers did with- an epoch in the history of the House of Gretton as out sleep or took it standing. Karakabad was '93 in the History of Europe. For months Madge like a buzzing hive; the first bees were already out. dated events from the time she was caught sitoking. The' day before Silly's battery started for the She took her father round the neck, front he had a surprise. He was with a young "Do, dad. Oh, I don't want to go back. I'm subaltern of the Second Essex Regiment, who was miserable that Billy is "one, and mother will be doomed to stay at the base, and, therefore, was too indigestible. Please let's go, and you can shew blaspheming when he saw a man—by his red me the things you like, that mother says I'm not shoulder-straps one of the staff—come swinging' to see. And I'll be good for months and meek as along on foot. a mouse." "Who's that Johnny, Greaves?" be asked. Of course, it ended in their goina ; for with "That ? Oh, it's Blundell, of the Punjab Madge attacking him, and no wife at hand fo keep Cavalry," growled Greaves. "He's acting him to his duty, George Gretton was easy to lead Quartermaster-General for your lot." in the way he wanted to go. "By-Jove " said Billy. "Why, I thought lie was They had a good. time there for three days, and a magistrate or some such thing at Dum-Dtim." once had Sir Dan to dine with them at ,the "So he was, but he's back with his regiment Bristol. Madge was brilliant and talkative, but as again. Lucky fellow t" nervous as a scared cat. She left them early, and I "I wonder how that happened." in the morning sent a telegram to India, which "Why?" asked Greaves. was partially in cipher and marked "private." "Because they had a down on him at Simla. And She Caine back to the Hotel and had a violent at home I know the Adjutant-General hates him. headache, a most unusual thing with her. When They say it was a row about a woman:" 1 she recovered she seemed to have lost all interest "Confound them all," said Greases, "women I in Paris. and the old women at Simla. Why shouldn't we "I think I want to go back, dad." go up too? Oh, it's a beastly shame! Do you By this time Mr. Gretton had received a letter know the A.G. at home, Gretton?" from his wife, and was not at all anxious to go Billy nodded. , home. Though he told her nothing about it Madge "Then that accounts for it," cried Greaves. guessed what was wrong. ,"By-and-bye you'll get as haughty as if you. "We may as well be hanged for a sheep as a belonged to the Goorklms." lamb," she said. "So, never mind, I'll stay as "What rot!" cried Billy. Did he not belong long as you like." to a mountain battery? They remained their week, and during all the He and Greaves had been at Marlborough time Madge suffered and triumphed; she was together or they might not have foregathered now. uplifted and cast down. She had committed a "What kind of chap is Blundell, really ?"•he crime, perhaps a legal crime. What would Sir asked,- mindful of Madge's high opinion of this Dan say and do when he found it out? And Black Devil. perhaps there was something wrong in the telegram "He's not popular, they say, but he's a thorough which would be held over for further instructions, soldier and knows every dashed hole in the beastly Co do it and get nothing for Richard after all country you've got to tackle," said Greaves. "And • would kill her. She grew pale and anxious, so , he knows all the bally languages as if he was a pale, indeed, that her father noticed it, and asked Guli, and a. Swati, and an Afghan hog too. I her questions. She said there was nothing wrong, suppose that's why he's back here. Heil be off and burst into tears with the utmost promptness. to-morrow." "It's B-B-illy," she sobbed, "I shall in-miss That night Blundell messed with Billy's battery, B-Billy." and if he had disliked the man on hearsay he took "My dear little girl," said Mr. Gretton, sympa- it all back before dinner was over; for Blundell, thetically. And these words added grief on grief who was on fire with joy at being again in a row and anxiety. "We'll go back at once." in any position, didn't seem to mind much that he "Yes, I want to go back," wailed Madge. was still only a squadron commander when by She was quite ready to be whipped with words rights he ought to have been second in command by her In-other, and she bore everything with such and well on for commandant. It seemed to Billy exemplary meekness that Mrs. Gretton was that Captain Blundell was specially taken with astonished, and trampled on her with great joy. him, and lie paid the compliment back in adore- But without revolt on her daughter's part the tion just as Madge had said he would. . pleasure palled, arid Madge's very meekness was a "Are you any relation of Gretton in the new inj miry. She was being treated with cold con- Sappers? asked Blundell late in the evening. tempt, with callous indifference, now, too, when He smiled as he asked it, for in his pocket was she had lost her b-b-boy, the only one in the house Madge's note. "Please don't tell Billy you know _ _ . ,</p>
        <p>who had ever loved her'. Madge listened and said: "Yes, mother," and all the time her mind was in India, or at the War Office, where she saw Sir Dan discovering what she had done. She dreaded the postman ; a telegram was a terror to her till she knew what it was about. And every day she tore open the newspapers to see if there was any word of Captain Blundell at the front, for now it was a case of war indeed. Seconder Afzul politely declined to coma out of it and carried his politeness to the extent of attempt- ing to provide Major Rawson and hi S escort with lodgings, thinking they would be desirable hostages in any further negotiation with the Indian authorities. The result of this was that Rawson and his men had rushed the Khan fort, and were now holding it, the Khan unfortunately being out- side with about seven thousand Gulistanis and an odd thousand or so of frontier ruffians who enjoyed a row and the cutting up of a few rash English- men above all things. So in India, while the English newspapers told them how to do it, the authorities set about organising a force to relieve Rawson. And Billy , was in a most furious state of mind. Would he get there in time, and would he get shoved into a mountain battery in time to go up, and oh! what a joke it would be if he was, and how other chaps would snort at the idea of his getting the fat while they had been in India for years never shooting anything more savage than a quail or a snipe. A terrible' idea struck Madge. Supposing Blundell met Bidy at Karakabad or Gilgit, or Quetta, or Peshawar, or somewhere, and indis- creetly let out, that he knew his sister 1 The very notion made her blood run cold. And then it ran hot, for she had quite a chance of writing a short, polite note to Richard begging him not to mention it. She turned pale and cold, and then rosy, and ran to her desk and'wrote twenty letters in ten mimates and one in twenty, and rash and careful ware alike torn into fragments to make way for a little bleat of anxiety. "Incase if you meet my brother Billy don't tell him you know me.-11,tuau GRETTON. 441.,.5.—1 hope you are well and not at Dtun- ?um any more. She wanted dreadfully to tell him what she had done. But she couldn t do that; he would think she was too fond of him. She sent one copy of her note to Drun-Dtun, and one to Gilgit, and (Me to Karakahad, and was very near sending a few more to Quetta and Rawul Pindi and Peshawar. But to do 'S9 would be to show most extreme anxiety, and she wouldn't do that, of course. And the next day Billy sailed. And Madge wept and said: "Oh, give all the dears my love, and be careful Billy, and don't shoot yonrself or do anything rash." • And Billy had a temporary cold in his eyes and</p>
        <p>me."' "Re's my uncle," answered Billy, with a blush. "Then you are of a good stbck, said Blundell. But he did not talk much with him. The others got him to tell them everything he knew of the passes and the country. And there was little he seemed not to know. "I hunted there when I was a subaltern," laughed Blundell, "and I've often been stalked by some confounded Pathan while I was stalking pme. It added interest to it, though." They knew there was no brag in what he said. if Lat Rapat was known as the right arm of the Sirkar from Gilgit to Quetta, in a smaller way Black Blundell had been a terror and wonder to the tribesman from the time he had taken notes in Swat disguised as an Afghan Mollah. But all the time Blundell talked and answerA questions he was wondering how it was that he Found himself back on the frontier again. And though at first he was naturally inclined to put it down to his qualifications and his knowledge Of* the country, he revised his opinion when he found I note addressed to hint at Karalmbad by a young tiady whom until then he had really entirely Forgotten, for who did slm know he would be there Y</p>
        <p>(To be continued.)</p>
        <p>said: - "Oh, dash it! Ciane now, I say—" and added other intelligible remarks of the same sort which betokened great perturbation of mind. And Mr. aretton said': "Run straight, my boy, and keep your end up and we'll all be proud of you." And Billy determined to do a deal more than that, and through the cold in his eyes he saw him- self a scarred and decorated Field Marshal, with all the letters of the alphabet tagged on to a most glorious name. He wrote an autubiography\i,n two large volumes, which ran through fifty edition4;--and took Afghanistan, and beat the Russians before the .steamer was well under weigh. And having seen Madge and his father fade into mist, though they danced about a bit before .they disappeared, he set about seeing wive he was. He knew before lom, for the brave boy that Mars could not have daunted, fell an easy prey to the god of the sea, and till he was thkough the Bay of Biscay he cared no more for honour and glory than he did for eggs and bas'. Not for the 'V.O. would he have rushed that pile of horrors, the -.breakfast-table, and what mind he had he devoted to wishing ho was dead. ' It was not till they swung round the Rock that ' he began to recover, and then as he lay in a &amp;el:- chair he looked as to colour for all the world like a poor fellow invalided home from Hong Kong after ten years without leave. He was glad Madge wasn't there to see him, for she would have picked him up and nursed him like a baby, and the bold voung gunner did not like the motion. Sea sick- ness was the most humiliating expdrience he had ever been through. He almost came to think that a sailor was something superhuman. By the time they arrived at Port Said he was pretty nearly his own man again. And there they got news of the Gulistan row. Rawson was still shut up, and it was touch•-and-go if the relief expedi- liiin got up in time to save him. So Billy fumed and the others fumed, and swore, and cursed the speed of steamships, and wanted to get out and walk though there was very little chande of anyone of them going up to the front. Yet, with a frontier seething, and one. row actually on, no one knew what worild happen. Before six Months Madge might really have half an t.rmy list on her books. It was the beginning of the "cold weather &gt;' in India when Billy ,resolied Bombay, and as he sweated ashore he wondered what the•deuce hot</p>
        <p>weather was like if that Was the -e01.d.--i;t-wi.;.a.t. lisrakabad he found the climate quite another thing. Ur had never been even in Switzerland, and now ifero lie was in the heart almost of the first chain of the Himalayas, with jackals waking him at nioitt by howls that would have disturber= a cemetery. And !I'm n “assan Amtat to Enrakahad he had been in a dream of wonder at the native life. Even the tortures of the Ekkas and tongas in which he drove were but a sauce piquant° to his diet. • But best of all and most glorious as to find dist the Gretton influence had worked, ae tqw</p>
        <p>-</p>
      </div>
