      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND</p>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Cricket.</p>
        <p>Warrington have still to play their first League match, as up to the present rain has interfered with their pragramme. On S tturday they should have met St. Helens Recs., but the game had to be abandoned. The second team, away from home, were more fortu- nate, and not only were they able to decide their match.but they won with ease. St. Helens batted first ,and totalled 40, to which Ainge contributed a useful 19. Callon was lar,gely responsible for their poor, display as, maintaining a good length, he took seven wickets for nine runs. The St. Helens total was reached with the loss of five wickets and the score was carried to 74 before Warrington were dismissed. T. Wright came out as the top scorer with 14.</p>
        <p>Walker's (Warrington) and Walker's (Burton) teams met on the Arpley ground on Saturday and victory rested with the wire-pullers whose total of 132 was seven times as large as that scored by their opponents. The Burton eleven cut a very sorry figtne against the bowling of Dargan and Norman who took teepee- lively four wickets for seven runs and six wickets for six runs. Dargan was equally successful with the bat and was 57 not out when play closed. E. Cook also batted well. for his 31.</p>
        <p>Warrington Secondary School on Saturday were defeated by the Widnes scholars. Batting first they were all dismissed for 20, whilst Widnes replied with 36.</p>
        <p>Penketh Recs. suffered a similar fate at the hands of St. Tames' second who improved by 0 runs upon their score of 20. Throughout the match the bowling triumiphed and the only man to reach double figures was Foulkes, who scored 11 for the winning team. -- -- Warrington preservers of the peace are enthusiastic cricketers and on Tuesday they met the local Co-operative eleven. Batting first they were unable to do anything with the bowling of Banner, who took six wickets at a cost of two runs each. The Co-operatives replied with 40, eight of their batsmen falling victims to Houghton's deliveries.</p>
        <p>A match between Cheshire Linee Engineers and Orford Barracks resulted in a win for. the soldiers. The Engineers, who had first knock, ran up a total of 99, to which Duckworth and Wright contributed respectively 25 and 221n0t out. To this the Barracks replied with 119 for nine wickets, White being top scorer with 35.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>Bowling.</head>
        <p>Bowling.</p>
        <p>Progress was on Saturday made in connection with the first round of the local works competition, and some excellent games were witnessed over the green attached to the Bowling Green Inn, Bank Quay. The least interesting of the matches was between Whitecross (No. 1) and Guests Tannery, as the wirepullers won with great ease, in fact, it looked at one time as though they were going to establish a record. With eight men off the green they had a lead of 79 points with the scores standing at 105-26, and it speaks well for the reminder of the tanners " ' that their advantage was only improved to the extent of 15 points.</p>
        <p>L. &amp; N.-W. and Crosfleld's (No. 2) were more evenly matched, and it was only after a good fight that the railway men qualified for the second round. E. Taylor and W. Bridge secured a useful lead, but the advantage was immediately lost, W. Bennett sending Crosfielcrs to the front by winning 15-4. Another win and a lose for the soap boilers saw the score carried to 65-60 in their favour, but immediately afterwards the teams peeled, and then the L. St N.-W. went to the front, retaining their advantage until the end, when eleven points separated the teams, despite the fact that their oppon- ents claimed 15 through the absence of J. Mercer.</p>
        <p>Individually some of the players had long fights and a good example was set by E. Taylnr and J. Middleton. The Warrington and District League secretary secured the first four ends and went to 8-2 when Middleton rose to the occasion and seven ends later peeled at 9. The majority of the next few points went to Taylor but the players again levelled up at 13. The last end was well fought but Taylor got two woods home by inches and so secured the victory. J. Willett and T. Horrobin had a still closer fight and peeled at 4,7, 12 and 14 before Willett was able to claim the game.</p>
        <p>Robinsons led throughout in their match with Rylands (No. 3) and yet at no period of the game was victory assured. After six ends four points separated the teams, and two games later the balance in favour of Robinsons was unaltered. R. Warburton improved matters by running away from J. Broadhurst the last named player only getting one wood home, but Robin- son's rejoicings were somewhat premature their lead being again reduced to five, by W. H. Thornley defeat- ing E. Leicester 15-2.</p>
        <p>J. Moss and J. Hutchinson next occupied the green and after a close game Hutchinson won 15-12, the totals standing at Robinsons 132, Rylands 121. It was left to G. Hands to establish a win which he did by defeating J. Allen 15-10. The concluding game was productive of considerable excitement as Allen at the outset went to the front only however to fall off later. He recovered towards the close hut his final effort was of no avail and Rylands had to bid]good bye to the chances of winning the trophy.</p>
        <p>The remaining game between Armitage and Rigby and Penketh Tannery completed the afternoon's pro- gramme and the fortunes of both teams fluctuated con- siderably before Penketh succeeded in winning. Armitage's went to the front as the result of the first four games, but the fifth saw the positions reversed. Again they secured the lead, only to lose it immediately and finally, and in the end Penketh won by 18 points.</p>
        <p>To-days matches are P. and K. (No. 1 team) v. Castle Rubber, Crosflelds (No. 1) v. Crosfleld's (No. 3), and Sankey Wire Mills v. Greenings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>Cycling.</head>
        <p>Cycling.</p>
        <p>Another run cancellec' ! Owing to the showery weather on Saturday afternoon the members of the Cycling Club did not embark on the journey to Cranage, and the record up to the present is eight run abandoned out of twelve fixtures. On Tuesday evening about twenty members turned out for a run to Glazebury and had a very enjoyable outing. On arrival at the Raven Hotel bowls were indulged in and afterwards an impromptu smoker was held.</p>
        <p>Will the clerk of the weather be kind this week end ? Cyclists are earnestly hoping so, for Whitsuntide is always a favourite holiday for tours. A large con- tingent of the Warrington Club have decided to visit Ellesmerl and the Vale of Llangollen. On Tuesday evening there will be the usual run to Tabley.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>We hear on very good authority that it is contem- plated to beautify Padgate Wesleyan Church, and that one of the alterations to the building will be the erec- tion of a small tower, of a very neat description. We I understand that the expense of the alteration will be borne by a member or members of the Bennett family es a thank offering and thus be a welcome gift to the Church.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>CHARGE AGAINST A FOOTBALLER.</head>
        <p>CHARGE AGAINST A FOOTBALLER.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn on Monday, Harry Palmer, a well-known Cheshire county footbalfer, was charged with assaulting a married woman named Lydia Parr, of 11, Rutland. street. The prosecutrix said that on Saturday night the 19th inst., she heard a knock at the door and immedi- ately she opened it the defendant struck her on the stomach and knocked her over two chairs. She had never spoken to the defendant in her life.—Cross- examined by Mr. Linaker (who defended), witness admitted that she had been drunk many a time, but was not on Saturday night. The defence was a complete denial of the assault. After hearing the evidence, the case was dismissed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
