      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, June 2, 1906. PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, June 2, 1906. PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>PVOM QUITE SUFFICIENT.</p>
        <p>According to a story recently circulated, the Ger- man Emperor suddenly appeared at six o'clock one. morning at the barracks of a dragoon regiment The soldiers were ready for manoeuvres, but the com- manding officer had not yet arrived. The delin- quent's feelings can better be imagined than described when he arrived and found the Emperor waiting for him. The ruler, however, did not say a word, but assisted at the manoeuvres, made several observations, as usual, and finally left without speaking to the officer, who by this time considered himself lost. lie went home in despair, and awaited his fate. But the afternoon passed, then the evening, and still nothing occurred. ;At last, late that same night, a little package was left at the house, addressed to the miserable man. No letter or message accompanied it. With frantic haste he tore off the wrapper of the parcel, and a hot flush mounted to his face as he realised the nature of the present: It was an alarm clock.</p>
        <p>Thereafter the Emperor had no reason to complain of his officer's unpunctuality. The hint was rite sufficient. \</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>BY STRATEGY.</head>
        <p>BY STRATEGY.</p>
        <p>Not long since a Yorkshire factory lad who was ex- ceedingly poor was obliged one morning to start away-to work without the customary dinner tied up in his handkerchief.</p>
        <p>Long before twelve o'clock had struck the boy was studying hard as to how be should procure the much needed meal, and soon an opportunity presented itself. The master of the mill usually came down about dinner time with his favourite dog, and it was not long before the lad happened to see it running about the yard.</p>
        <p>Seizing a large stick that was lying near, he im- mediately commenced chasing the animal round the place, culling out: " hi! Put it down! Put it down !"</p>
        <p>The master, catching sight of what we.s going on, quickly came on the scene for an explanation, and the boy's ready answer wag: " He s not going to take my dinner for nothing, I can tell ye, sir." . .</p>
        <p>, The master, without the slightest hesitation, gave the lad a shilling tcP replace what he, of course, thought his dog had taken, and the artful youngster was able to purchase a good meal. He was wise enough, however, to refrain frow repeating the fraud.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>ma FIRST LESSON.</head>
        <p>ma FIRST LESSON.</p>
        <p>A captain of a training vesseltells the story of his first experience of the sea. He had but just come on board of the schooner where, as cabin-boy, he was to serve his apprentice- ship, arid was still staring about him with boyish in- terest and inquisitiveness, when the skipper ap- proached and ordered hitn to assist in washing down the deck.</p>
        <p>He started awkwardly to do so, when a second order was given him, to, take off his shoes and stockings. Hs was perfectly willing to do so; hut at home he had not been permiited to we, his feet:</p>
        <p>"No." he answered innocently, with an engaging smile, "I should not mind; but my mother does not allow it."</p>
        <p>- • - The slipper was a rough old sea-dog who di rl not appreciate obedience unless it was rendered lo self, and his reply was a stunning blow dint flung the boy across the deck."</p>
        <p>11t after that," the narrator says with not a shadow of resentment, "I knew very well who was the captain of that schooner; and it wasn't my mother."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>118 rAL.</head>
        <p>UN 118 rAL.</p>
        <p>An ox is about the last animal which would be looked upon as a performer of considerable activity, or a sheep as one from which to expect cotn.ageolis fighting, and yet, as the following proves, %hat is imexpticied occasionally happens. A lieniknant of a cavalry regiment in Berlin succeeded in so training art oz in six months th,tt it, would obey the word of command like a nara!ry horse. At a recent trial of its powers. which took place On a drill ground, the ox trotted, 'galloped right., galloped left, and imitated the more in aloio-t everything, except vaulting, which was beyond its capacity. The officer was highly complimented on his perseverance and success to training so unpromising an animal. -</p>
        <p>The plucky sheep in question was found at the Calcutta Zoological Gardens, where it accidentally came into contact with a tiger. To the keeper's avonishiaent, however, the sheep. being cf a plignac•cus disposition, attacked the tiger, and, after a sharp tussle, killed it.</p>
        <p>PROM A CANNON UT VTR SRY.</p>
        <p>Much has been written of the perils of those daring acrobats who mount skywards. swinging front a balloon, and then ,icseeto.t to v..;li by the aid of a pt rachtit e. So far. however, none oF them has passed through such a urrible et perreoet3 as a parachutist named Burke, who is accustomed to fire himself from riinuon attached to hie balloon, and then descend with his parachute in the customary way. The ot her dtly while irig in his c.•nnort, which tie balloon had carried high in i he Cr, Burke tumid tluat, owing to some hitch in the appareAlls, he coal not operate the coiled spring wh;,,h shoots hno fro", the cannon. and, as a conseqoence, the balloon haviog reached the limit of its ascent was beginning to shrink and descend rapidly. It was a frightful situation, but the athlete kept his nerve and began to crawl out of the • cannon. Faster and faster the balloon shot down, and once the man narrowly escaped slipping off the cannon as he emerged from its mouth. A rope hanging from the balloon, however, saved him ; the parachute partly opened, and a fee seconds later he had cut loose from the balloon and dropped into the branches of a friendly pine tree. But it was "a close share—perhaps as narrow an escape from death as any man could well have.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>MADE_ HIM UNDERSTAND.</head>
        <p>MADE_ HIM UNDERSTAND.</p>
        <p>A gentleman who has had a great -deal to do with the wiles of Chinamen tells the following to illus- trate a well-known characteristic of John, which is never to understand anything which he does not wish to understand. No man on earth can be denser than he when he chooses. Some time back a Chinaman was summoned for being in possession of a tenement unfit for human habitation. The case was clearly proved, and he was fined One pound. But in no way could John be made to underetand that a fine had been inflicted. He sat there with unmoved stolidity, and all the court could extractlrom him was :</p>
        <p>"Me no savey—no savey." After saying this in a voice devoid of all hope, he sank again into silence. Then rose a well-known lawyer. "With your worship's permission, I think I can make the Chinaman understand," he said.</p>
        <p>Ile was.pertilitted to try. Striding fiercely up to the Celestial, he said to him in a loud voice: " John, you're tined two pounds." " No fear l—only ono!" was the sharp retort.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>TAKES SURP. REVENGE.</head>
        <p>TAKES SURP. REVENGE.</p>
        <p>There is only one creatute that will track you down to avenge the death of itspate, no matter how far you go, and that is a snake. Moreover, it -a the most terrible of all snakes and the largest of the poisonous variety, the hammiryad. A bite from this reptile %%ill ic,ll the strongest man in two minutes and there is no kiicwn remedy. No one bitten by n:hatnadrynal ha-, ever survived.</p>
        <p>They generally go about, in couples, and if you kill one of then) the other in all probability will be quickly on your track. Short of taking ship across the sea, there is no way of escaping unless you watch for tl2e snake and shoot it. _</p>
        <p>It Will follow you for miles for the chance of a stealthy stroke, and you may climb trees or mountains. or ford rivers, but there is no evasion of the hama- dryad. It is a sort of nightmare hunt to be chased by one, and when the chase begins either one or the other will have to die within a day or so. The mate of the slain snake never makes a mistake, but always kills the slayer of its companion, and him or her, as the case may he. alone. This reptile grows to a length of fifteen feet some- times, and is fairly common in Malay and New Guinea. One scratch from its fangs is certain and instant death.</p>
        <p>A : "FRICKLESNIIIII is a very versatile man, isn't he?" B: " Oh, yes? He makes a different kind of fool of himself almost every day in the week." SIIE "Why does that piano sound first loud and then soft when Bella plays it?" : "Well, you see, she's just learning to ride a bicycle, and uses both pedals from force of habit." Sracl: MANAGER (to lessee of theatre) : "Our scene- shifter wants a holiday. He says he hasn't been away for three years." Lessee : "Well, tell him he can't have one." He gets change of scenery enough here.' CONVERSATION is an art practised chiefly by those who are not skilled in it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>"SWEATED INDUSTRIES AND DRUGGED PEOPLES."</head>
        <p>"SWEATED INDUSTRIES AND DRUGGED PEOPLES."</p>
        <p>REV. JOHN YONGE'S SERMON ON CHRISTIAN RESPONSIBILITY. Resolution at Wycliffe Church</p>
        <p>"Christian responsibility for sweated industries and drugged peoples" was the subject of a sermon by the Rev. John Yonge at Wycliffe Congregational Church on Sunday evening. Taking for his text the words : "Undo the heavy burdens; let the oppressed go free; and break every yoke" (Isaiah Iviii., 6) the preacher said that for the purpose of that address, any number of Scripture words offered themselves as appropriate texts. Perhaps the sentences just read comprised as accurate descriptions of the degrading and distressed human conditions that would come under notice, and as clear definition of Christian duty in regard to them as could be desired. The prophet was making a powerful, even impassioned appeal to his people concerning the practical irreligion of their religiousness. He was bidden to tell them that they were contradicting their devotions before God by their malpractices towards their fellow men. By some strange perversity of idea, they were fasting, as though penitent before God,while at the very time they were oppressing and starving and impoverishing their helpless and poor. Men could not be right with God while they were wrong with one another. Heaven would not condone offences against humanity because of any amount of prayers and penances which the offenders might offer. The prophet described the religious observance which was alone acceptable. The people could only put themselves right with God if first they made their relations right with their fellows. He proclaimed the essential divine doctrine of common humanity—proclaiming it even to God's own Israel in a time when the people had sunk to the cruel level of the heathen nations in their despisal and oppression of the helpless and the poor. He (the preacher) wanted to say that night that this doctrine needed for many reasons to be reiterated and enforced upon Christian England in this twentieth century. It had unquestionable fitness for our own day and our own nation. There were indeed better signs now appearing of an awakening and spread- ing sense in this country of a</p>
        <p>HUMANITARIAN SPIRIT —a spirit of consideration for the helpless and down- trodden, a spirit not only of compassion and condescen- sion, but of desire to seek after and work for right relations between social classes and different nations. It was one of the paramount duties of the Christian Church and the Christian pulpit to further and foster all opinions and movements which were on that line. Do not let them make any mistake! The humanitarian spirit was the Christian spirit. It was the essentially religious spirit. It came of the true love of goodness and of God. So long and so far as professedly Christian people or Christian nations • denied and contravened true humanity, they were deny- ing and contravening Christianity. He wanted to call attention to two conspicuous instances in which this Christian nation, to whick we belonged, was seriously and even flagrantly violating the true spirit of humanity. One case was our imperial conduct towards a foreign nation. The other was our industrial treatment of unprotected and helpless workers in our labouring classes. Public attention was being specially 'directed to both those national inflictions of wr-ng at this very time—and hence his remarks on the subject. Probably many who had seen the phrase "drugged peoples " in the title of the sermon would have some- what wondered what it alluded to. They might have asked what we as Christian people could have done or could be still doing to justify such an expression. The explanation, however, was only too sadly and too terribly accurate. Of course it found some degree of explanation if we said that we had often, in reality, been " drugging peoples " or native races by the atrocious traffic in alcoholic liquor which Christian England had carried on in various parts of the uncivilized world. A very large amount of human misery had been created by that traffic, as it had been promoted from this and other so-called civilized and Christian countries. It was not of that, however, that bespoke now. Indeed that traffic had been privately carried on as a commercial specula- tion. But he had to speak of a traffic in a pernicious drug, carried on under the direct sanction of the Government of this Christian country and which had even been forced upon an unwilling State by British policy and British arms, and was to-day being upheld and legalised by treaty relations with the country where this trade was carried on. He was referring, of course, to the traffic in opium with China. The occasion for alluding to it was in the fact that on the following Wednesday in the House of Commons Mr. Theodore C. Taylor, M.P., for the Radcliffe division of Lancashire, would move a resolution in condemnation of this trade and asking for its being brought to an early and complete termination. The traffic had been opposed even from its very beginning by Christian people in this country, but never before had so large a probability of successful opposition existed as now, when it was believed that a majority of members in the House would vote far the suppression of the traffic. He was hoping that, at the Church meeting to be held that evening their members would pass a resolution in support of the proposal—as that was being considerably done in the country—so that the Government might understand that the opinion ol:the Churches was in favour of this _ NATIONAL INIQUITY _ - -</p>
        <p>coming to an end. Opium, the preacher explained, was the prepared juice of the poppy. The plant was grown in British India under the direct control of the Govern- ment, for export to China. It was made into opium at Government factories, the great bulk being sold to opium merchants who sent it to China. The population of China was 400,000,000, and amongst this vast people. opium smoking now most widely and disastrously prevailed. MI Chinese people themselves declared that opium was a curse. All Christian missionaries were against it and they testified that the conscience of all China was opposed to the habit, classing it with immorality and gambling. It was debasing, demoraliz- ing, and enslaving in its effects, being the worst form of intoxication. In the eighteenth century opium smoking was prohibited by the government of China and the trade in opium was forbidden. After constant and persistent warnings to the English merchants engaged in the trade, the Chinese Government in 1839 seized all the opium then lying in ships in Chinese waters, destroying it to the value of two -millions sterling. Thereupon war ensued. As the result five Chinese ports were compulsorily opened :by force of British arms to the traffic, and China had to pay the two , millions of money for the opium with four millions more to this country for the cost of the war. In 1857 a second Nvar occurred and more Chinese ports were opened. In 1869 the Chinese Government again sought to stop the trade and pleaded with Great Britain in vain. Negotiations against the trade had been frequent since that date, but this country had always persisted in its treaty rights on the selfish and un-Christian ground that to stop the traffic would be a heavy financial loss to our Indian Empire. Everybody knew that the opium habit was</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>A. CURSE TO HUMANITY</head>
        <p>A. CURSE TO HUMANITY</p>
        <p>and that it was the especial curse of China to-day, and also that it was the greatest obstacle to the cause of Christianity in China—and vet, in spite of all, the fact remained that this great Christian Empire of Great Britain 'held the vast empire of China to its bond— inflicting thereby the most absolute mental and moral bondage upon its millions of people—because to stop the traffic would make a great loss to the finances of India! We went on drugging the people of China in order to fiud money to rnaintainand govern our Empire in India. We were the richest empire in the world; we called ourselves by the sacred name of Christ, and this • was the policy and the treat- ment which we maintained with the "heathen Chinee." The effects of the opium habit were infinitely worse than those of alcohol. And yet we fastened it upon those treaty-bound Chinese without relenting. If this was not " smiting with the fist of wickedness " what was? Christian Britain had heathen China by the throat in this business, and she seemed to mean that the bondage was going to last as long as she chose. Hardly, however I China was slowly but surely emerging from her long pupilage and bondage, and it might be that when she herself struck once more for freedom she might secure it at a far greater loss to us than that of money. It was no exaggeration to say that the whole history of our opium trade with China was</p>
        <p>A SCANDAL AND A REPROACH to our nation, and that it invited the judgmentsof God. If we British Christians were sincere in our desire to Christianise China, we should become even eager to liberate her fiom this bondage of evil which our Christian hands had so long fastened upon her. The preacher quoted the testimony of Griffith John, the veteran missionary in China, who said the trade had been forced by England upon the Chinese in direct opposition to the moral convic- tions of the nation as well as the honest and long con- tinued resistance of the Government, and that opium smoking was an unmitigated curse to the Chinese, a curse physically, morally and socially to both the nation and the individual. " I do not see "he added "how any enlightened follower of Jesus Christ can have two opinions as to what we as a nation ought to do. Eng- land's relation to the opium traffic is a national dis- grace." In leaving this part of his subject, Mr. Yonge said that difficult as the policy of abandoning the traffic might be, and financially costly as it might be to the Indian empire, a Christian nation could be no more light in doing wrong than a Christian individual could be. The man or the nation that attempted to justify throng by the excuse that it led to right, stood con- demned at the Bar of the Righteous Gad. With regard to the subject of Christian responsibility for</p>
        <p>SWEATED INDUSTRIES, this was not a case of our national com- plicity in wrong-doing, oppression and binding burdens upon people on the other side of the globe. It was a ease of those very things here, at home, in our own land. Those who tead newspapers must be more, or less familiar with the weird and weary tales of hard labour at starvation wages—of men, women and even children slaving from morning till midnight for wages hardly sufficient to keep soul and body together. The tale seemed to have become so familiar as to have lost its</p>
        <p>power to impress either imagination, heart or will. But this month in a public hall in the West End of London, the " Daily News" proprietors had organized a very remarkable exhibition. It consisted of a series of compartments in which were working people busily engaged in following the occupations which usually they pursued in their own squalid homes. All these occupations were samples of the industries which were "sweated "—that was the workers, not being pro- tected by any trade or labour union, carried on their work at home at the entire mercy of employers who paid the poorest and barest possible wage—a wage so small that only by the longest hours and the most persistent and exhausting labour could the workers sustain life at all. The exhibition was a philanthropic and benevolent expedient, designed to compel the attention of all classes of the nation to the wretched conditions int which thousands of the poorest were co imelled to live. Very few of the workers represented at the exhibition made as much as 2d. an hour. The usual rate was from 141. to 1;d. an hour, and in some cases workers had to find some of their materials, such as thread, paste, &amp;c. Mr. Yonge then gave a list of occupations and the extraordinarily low wages paid. One of the most appealing exhibits was that of the makers of confirmation wreaths—a trade principally for export to foreign countries. The wreaths contained about a gross of small white flowers, and the wire stem had to be cove,-ed with white lawn, intertwined with silver leaves (which were sup- plied to the worker) and the whole shaped into a complete chaplet. One shilling and ninepence a dozen , was the price paid—labour 12 hours a day. The price paid for children's clothing could be judged from the fact that Princess Henry bought a white, daintily made baby's pelisse trimmed and lined throughout,marked to sell in a shop at ;11s. 6d. The woman who made it ' received tenpence. A shoemaker who made carpet slippers sold at 2s. 6d. per pair received 2s. 6d. per dozen:pairs, and the materials he had to find cost 6d. A 'roman covered men's umbrellas at Is. 6d. a dozen, paying for her own thread, each umbrella fetching is, lid. A woman made boys' knickers at Is. 10d. a dozen, earning an average of 6s. a week. A woman and her husband made artificial flowers. For fifty years she had been at it. Her husband was now nearly blind and quite an invalid. They bad lived in an underground room, rent 4s. 6d. They made violets at 7d. a gross ; buttercups at 3d. ; roses is. 3d. to 35.6 d. a gross. Every petal must be perfect or it would be rejected. , A book folder, who had broken down under the strain of her work, folded Bibles and Prayer Books,at one penny per 100 sheets:Z.3r making a ! costume selling at 2Ss. 11d., the wage paid was 10d. ; for another selling at 313. 6d. the worker received is. At box-making it took a woman 12 hours incessant work to earn fifteen pence. Trousers could be made at 4.d. a pair, coats at 10d., a suit for Is. 3d. Having quoted other particulars, Mr. YONG E continued : And all this went on in a highly civilized land which boasted of its Christianity and called itself THE HOME OF FREEDOM. What freedom did those people enjoy? What faith were they encouraged to have in religion? To look at their wan faces and bent shoulders and to note the ' agonised intentness with which they pushed on with their work, knowing that it alone stood between them and starvation, was enough to make a thoughtful specta- tor weep. "Poor things ! How can they do it " was what everybody exclaimed as they passed round the room. Pity was plentiful enough and cheap enough too. But the exhibition did not aim at mere commiseration. It aimed at setting people thinking and feeling and resolving and doing something to set that dreadful condition right. It was time to stop pitying and to begin reforming. As Canon Scott Holland had said " Pity that was of no practical us. murdered." The question Christian people had to con- sider was "What is Chrisi,ian responsibility in this matter? Are these poor oppressed toilers our brethren ? If so are we their keepers? Is their sorrow any con- cern of ours ?" Sermons were being preached about the sin of cheapness. They all knew what a fascination there was about a thing which was cheap. There was no thought of the brother and sister humans who had been sweated to produce it. Christian people went about dressed up in some of those</p>
        <p>CHEAP WONDERS exultant in their comely apparance, never dreaming of the toil-worn fingers and the starving stomachs of the people who had made th.m. That cheapness was a ghastly business. The thing on that grave subject which needed to be done was to compel all classes of the nation to think and realise their own responsibility for it. That was supremely a matter for thought and prayer and the endeavour of religious people. It was even a far more crying necessity than whether creed and catechism were to he taught in day schools. Anglican and Roman Churches were rampant upon that and Non- conformists were rampant on the other side. But the thousands of the sweated workers were little remem- bered, little realized, little cared for. He asked whether that chapter in the ancient prophecy was not timely and urgent in its practical condemnation and appeal. What was to be said of the Christian nation which could drag and degrade a heathen people by treaty and could enslave and sweat thousands of its own citizens, making</p>
        <p>LIFE A MISERY AND DEATH A DELIVERANCE. We were responsible for the welfare of our fellow citizens We ought not to tolerate any state of things by which we could pay the costs of our Indian Empire at the expense of degraded millions, and countless, needless premature deaths in China. Nor should we tolerate a commercial and industrial system which meant a killing slavery to thousands of our people—bare starvation, dirt and death to men women and children. Our Christian duty was to take such measures as would undo the heavy burdens, let the oppressed go free and break - ing every yoke. " Then shall thy light break forth as the morning and thine health shall spring forth speedily and thy righteousness shall go before thee. The glory of. the Lord shall be thy reward."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>OPIUM TRAFFIC.</head>
        <p>THE OPIUM TRAFFIC.</p>
        <p>Resolution of Protest.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the members of the above church. held after the snwice it was unanimously resolved :- 1. That this meeting desires.% put on record its sense of the incalculable evils, physical, moral and social, which continue to be wrought in China through the Opium Trade, that it deeply deplores the position occupied by Great Britain through its Indian administration in the growth, manufacture and sale of opium ; that it recognises clearly that nothing short of the entire suppression of the trade from India can meet the claims of the case ; and that it now appeals to the Government to take immediate steps to bring it to an end. 2. That copies of the foregoing resolution he sent to the Prime Minister, the Secretary of State for India, and the Member of Parliament for the Borough.</p>
        <p>DEATH IN THE MINE AT HAYDOCIi. The inquest on William Gorman, aged forty-five, day- wageman, of 26, Queen-street, St. Helens,who died from injuries sustained at the Parr pits of Messrs. R. Evans and Co., was held at the Waggon and Horses Hotel, Haydock, on Wednesdsy by Mr. Brighouse, county coroner. The coroner explained the plan, produced by Mr. Peace, and said that when the alarm was given, and the roof in the working place was heard " crunching " or cracking, the three men who were in the place ran out, but while the two escaped Gorman unfortunately ran right under the place where the roof was coming down. It was natural to make a dash for safety, but Gorman was unlucky in the way he ran.l The jury returned a verdict of " Accidental death,''and expressed sympathy with the'vridow and family of the deceased.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>*UMW A</head>
        <p>*UMW A</p>
        <p>Soap, Ointment, and Pills the World's Greatest Skin Cures.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>COMPLETE TREATMENT</head>
        <p>COMPLETE TREATMENT</p>
        <p>For Every Humour, from Pimples to Scrofula, from infancy to Age.</p>
        <p>The agonizing itching and burning of the skin, as in eczema; the frightful scal- ing, as in psoriasis; the loss of hair and crusting of the scalp, as in scalled head; the facial disfigurement, as in pimples and ringworm; the awful suffering of infants and the anxiety of worn-out parents, as in milk crust, tetter, and salt rheum, all demand a remedy of al- most superhuman virtues to successfully cope with them. That Cuticura Soap, Ointment, and Pills are such stands .oroven beyond all doubt. No statement is made regarding them that is not justi- fied by the strongest evidence. The purity and sweetness, the power to afford immediate relief, the certainty of speedy and permanent cure, the absolute safety and great economy, have made them the standard skin cures and humour reme- dies of the civilized world.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>YEARS OF TORTURE</head>
        <p>YEARS OF TORTURE</p>
        <p>Itching and Painiul Sores Covering tlead and Body Cured By Cuticurn.</p>
        <p>"For fifteen years my scalp and fore- head was one mass of scabs, and, my body was covered with sores. Words cannot express how I suffered from the itching and pain. I 1:14d given up hope when a friend told me to get Cuticura. After bathing with Cuticura Soap and applying Cuticura Ointment for three days, my head was as clear as ever, and to my sur- prise and joy, one cake of soap and one box of ointmenbmade a complete cure in one week. (signed) H. B. Franklin, 717 Washington St., Allegheny, Pa."</p>
