        <p>Grocery r I</p>
        <p>WHI'ItUNTIDE SAILINGS. Tn cornection with the Tedcastle Line, we observe that the modern tendency to include Hotel catering for tourists has been adopted, and, for an extremely low inclusive fare week-end and short date excursion tickets are issued, covering return Saloon fare to Dublin, meals on board, and hotel accommodation there. Similarly, the weekly 6 days tour per S.S. " Cumbria " to Whit haven every Saturday night for the English Lakes, offers great inducements to pleasure seekers. This trip gives about 31 days in the English Lake district, as well as 1 to 11 days in Dublin, and is deservedly popular with tourists and cycling parties. On Fridays and Saturdays, cheap excursion bookings are announced to Killarney, Cork, &amp;c., and at Killarney, hotel accommodation is provided if desired. No one need miss the enjoyment of a trip to the Capital of Ireland, seeing that it may be made by the Tedcastle steamers at the extremely low return fares of 10s. saloon, or ss. steerage.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>LIVERPOOL AND NORTH WALES WHITSUNTIDE ARRANGEMENTS.</head>
        <p>LIVERPOOL AND NORTH WALES WHITSUNTIDE ARRANGEMENTS.</p>
        <p>The Liverpool and North Wales Steamship Company have issued their sailing arrangements for Whitsuntide holidays, from which it will be observed that the favourite palatial saloon paddle steamer La Marguerite will take up the daily sailings on Saturday, 2nd, June leaving Liverponl for Llandudno, Beaumari,s Bangor, and Menai Bridge, allowing a short stay there, and returning to Liverpocl in the evening. There are also numerous extra sailings for Whit-Sunday and Whit- Monday, particulars of which are advertised. Should there be anything like fine weather there is no doubt the passenger accommodation will be fully taxed at Whitsuntide. Week-end coupoNs are issued entitling the holder to hotel accommodation and steamboat travelling at week-ends, particulars of which can be obtained at the company's office in Liverpool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>Haven't Time.</head>
        <p>Haven't Time.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. Stewart Royston has a short article in "Good Words "on the above text. The article opens as follows : It is strange that whenever a noble band of self-sacrificinz workers appeal to those in the possession of perfect health and ample leisure to come forward and help by their presence the work of some praiseworthy canse, the appeal is invariably met with the words, " Haven't titne." is it oftener than not a false but more truly a selfish excuse which finds utterance in these words? " Time Nv ai ts for no one,", that we all know, but it does not speed by too quickly for each one of us to perform what is his or her duty. " Haven't time !" The business man does not (Ise these words when on entering the office he finds piles of letters awaiting his perusal. The lawyer does not excuse himself thus when clients await an interview. Nor does the physician when his numerous patients are to be visited, any more than does the industrious mother when the torn garments of her little ones need her care and attention. All in their station of life " make "time.</p>
        <p>GARNETTS' FURNITURE</p>
        <p>INEXPENSIVE.</p>
        <p>V P-TO-DATE.</p>
        <p>GARNETTS' FURNITURE</p>
        <p>INEXPENKVE.</p>
        <p>UP-TO-DATE.</p>
        <p>GARNETTS' FURNITURE</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>17P-TO-DATiC.</head>
        <p>17P-TO-DATiC.</p>
        <p>INEXPENSIVE.</p>
        <p>GARNETTS' ' FURNITURE</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>EST 1824.</p>
        <p>IT is evident that the THE OPPOSITION opp9sition intends to die- AND cusi the Finance Bill at THE BUDGET, what is known as "long- lengths." That is not because it has a great deal to say on this or any other subject. There has never been a more feeble Opposition. We make the remark without any reference to numbers. The feebleness is more peculiarly in political equipment, in knowledge of affairs, in settled convictions of any sort whatever; but one quality the Opposition undoubtedly possesses—it is not capable of being abashed, it shrinks from nothing, it is not ashamed of being openly insincere. It is not difficult to conceive that a capable Opposition under good leadership might put f arward some very effective criticism of the Budget. But of leadership there is practically none. The Front Bench men come and go at short or long intervals. They are like the wind, which bloweth where it listeth. Their followers are not followers at all ; they are all guerilla captains. There seems to be no general instruction except to embarrass the Government. or, to speak the truth more accurately and put the case on a lower level, to waste the time of Parliament. There were some topical illustrations of this' contemptible system when the House of Commons on Tuesday went into Committee on the Finance Bill. We shall confine our remarks to only one of the subjects raised. This is the tea duty. Mr. COURTHOPE, a young Conservative member who , has capacity for hesitating through long speeches, moved, in the words of the order paper, that.. "Clause 1., page 1, line 20, leave out tivepence' and insert fourpence." This looks unintelli- gible, no doubt, but it was really a proposal to upset the Budget by taking twopence off the tea duty instead of a penny, as Mr. ARQUITH proposed. The CHANCELLOR. OF THE Excusquca had an easy reply, of course. He would only be too glad to take twopence off the tea duty if he had the means. Would hon. mem- bers opposite show him the way? Did they pro- ' pose that nothing should be devoted to the reduc- tion of debt ? Of course not; they proposed nothing whatever. They had no sort of suggestion to make except that the CHANCELLOR OF ran EXCHEQUER should take his Budget to pieces and reconstruct it, whereupon they would be ready with precisely the same kind of futile—and we shall say foolish—opposition that is now being offered.</p>
        <p>Mr. COIIRTHOPE made the surprising statement considering its source, that all taxes on such articles as tea are wholly indefensible. That has been the Liberal argument for time out of mind. The Liberal party had been contending for a period beyond which no existing memory goes back for a free breakfast table ; and it has never been able to secure a free breakfast table because of the operation of "the law of the pendulum.' The Conservatives come into power after an epoch of Liberal economy and rush. the country into heaps of new debt, so that when the Liberals return to the control of affairs they are unable to dispense with obnoxious taxation, because they have to bear the burdens of their predecessors. But the Conservatives have never believed in a "free breakfast table." Until Tuesday it was never said from the Conservatives benches that the, tea duty is indefensible. The doctrine, as was said by a member on the Govern- ment side of the House, is a Liberal egg in a Tory nest. But undeGits pret.ent conditions the Conservative party has no fear of new opinions. It will adopt any opinions whatever, if by doing so it can make it appear to the country that " Codlin is the friend, not Short." The endeavour to figure as the friend of the people with regard to the tea duties was particularly impudent and offensive. We are inclined to think also that it was short sighted. The public must surely resent a course of Parliamentary conduct which is based on the idea that it has no money.</p>
        <p>A question of paramount A Muon-NEEDEn importance to the workers REFORM, of the country is land reform. Strenuous efforts are being made by specially-formed organizations to educate public opinion on the subject and we can only regret that there was not a large attend- ance of Warrington, people to hear the admirable lecture delivered last Friday evening by Mr. W. R. Lester, M.A. He presented his arguments in a calm and convincing style, not,in-dulging in any tirades at th 3 expense of land owners but urging his case by sheer force of facts. The non-taxation of land valueE,he clearly showed ,lies at the root of many of our social evils. To everyone who thinks a little the injustice of the present system must be evident. The value of land increases as the result of industrial enterprise and the owner, while real,' ing a harvest, gives practically nothing return to the community which has created the additional wealth. When land is required f°r public improvements exhorbitant prices are demanded, and it can readily be seen that progrola in the direction of housing and other reforms le, thus seriously retarded. The ,monopoly in lan'</p>
        <p>P</p>
        <p>II</p>
        <p>, I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <p>sa col the of of t. seat. Th tiona, yours doth c steal." new. 1' eaterec The " travel. mother f tolerated was rigic ing her p many rule The Iv( Society des The exhibil was brough rapidly wer walls and di for ordinary The Ches been agitated girls plaiting Girls School . hey went to Mr. T. Woollat viously warned children should plaits. Sankey-street, This is merely a suit. The actual money has been Anyhow the new the old. A cyclis it is much softer ti clothes in conseque Corporation will pc The speeches of of the trial of a Wm Sessions this week. trast in styles. W prosecution, unfolded way, Mr. Tobin K.C. and impassioned appe on the pathetic. Eac. and created a great im We hear that then thing definite being Vol of Warrington Bridge. inittee recently appointe report on the method ferro-concrete principle understand, are about t which have been constri Chairman will probably g Thursday's Council meeti. The consolidation of effol Extension Scheme leaves necessary funds will soon are private subscribers be working men at some of t women are also putting a si another column, Mrs. Picl Infirmary Ladies' Committee from the women and asks fo will help. We are sure tht ponse. * * * The Rev. John Yonge did At the back of the Warrin s special e for the "le well to emphasize on Sun- -1" C‘ orrupt and Illegal Practice day our individual and 1501, Mr. Samuel Gough has national .responsibility with himself, but many people have p 4"ard t "sweated • dustries and drugged oinshortly have to share the hon ,Ppoples. The spirit of the people needs rousing, Peacock. We are informed, ho if a stop is to be put to the enormities perpetrated viction of the latter under the Under their noses and in their name. It is not of 1888 though it disqualifies h merely a Christian responsibility, but a claim on public Office for seven years, does the hearts of all whether professed Christians, as a voter. agnostics, or atheists. The claim is that of com- mon humanity, and we need to consider oftener If some tram tickets are worth what "men's inhumanity to man" is capable of Cheltenham causing. None of us can plead ignorance of the • fact that in certain trades the god of cheap ' ness is propitiated by terrible sacrifices of human life and health and happiness. And Yet, as Mr. Yonge pointedly remarked, "all this in a land which boasted of its Christianity and called itself the home of freedom." The remedy is , difficult, hut it can be made easy and certain if People will rouse themselves to a sense of their ipdividual responsibility and din their cries of Shame " often and ever oftener into the ears of those who are charged with the Government of the country and the welfare of the nation. ' Its difficult to realise that we Euglish—the Iniost civilized and humanitarian people on the 4 11°be—bolster up such a trade as that in opium, What is still worse compel the Chinatnan to our pernicious products. Were the opium t. de in England it would have had a short shrift a"„ 411 probability, but India is far off, and we are a„P,t to forget that we are the responsible governors Proprietors of that great country unless there Is Et rumour of Russian aggression on the frontier. „tl5 quite time that the national iniquity of cur -Plum trade with China was put an end to, and Pieasing to relate, there have been indications 11l Parlimnent this week that such a happy consummation is at hand. Fortunately 11% have to-day, as secretary for India, onA of the greatest hearl-ed and high-minded Of English politicians—Nlr. John Morley—who as the result of the protest, raised in the Commons ! Pl'prnieed that if the Chinese Government are still , concerned for the removal of the evil it shall he removed, though it would involve a large sacrifice. This is as much as could be expected in the way of a deelaration from a Minister of the Crown, and we trust it is but the forerunner of such an alteration of our relations with China as shall retnove for ever the stain which has for so many Years associated with the British name.</p>
        <p>* * *</p>
        <p>* * * At the back of the Warrington burgess roll there is a special page for the "legion of honour," headed " Corrupt and Illegal Practices List." Since June 1904, Mr. Samuel Gough has had the page all to himself, but many people have presumed thatshe would shortly have to share the honour with Mr. Arthur Peacock. We are informed, however, that the con- viction of the latter under the Corrupt Practices Act of 1888, though it disqualifies him from holding any public office for seven years, does not di4franchise him</p>
        <p>a sovereign each, those issued by the and District Light Railway ought to be worth more. On the back of one is the following couplet : "Many ills are cured by tramway pills, t Consisting only of tram rides out to the hills."</p>
        <p>Another ticket goes into details :- 4 If you are full and weary of troubles you would like to leave, Jump on the tram and ride out to the hills at Cleeve." * * * _ _</p>
        <p>The idea is one which might he emulated locally whenever the space on the back of the tram tickets happens to be vacant. We will undertake to supply the necessary " poetry " equal to the Cheltenham variety. For instance the following might prove attractive :</p>
        <p>If the state of your health is making you groan, Just ride out to Longford and try the ozone. • * * - A peculiar rating anomaly was disclosed at Newton on Thursday when a local farmer named Williams was summoned for the payment of 345. water rate by the Urban District Council of Newton-in-Makerfield. The defendant protested that as he was not supplied with town's water it was an abominable charge upon him as well as other farmers. The Chairman said they could not help defendant in the matter, and made an order for payment. Apparently an inquiry is necessary for it is certainly an injustice if people have to pay for water which they never use. In view of the American meat-packing revelations special interest lies just the fact that the Local Govern- ment Board have granted a Provisional Order enabling the Manchester Port Sanitary Authority to exercise more stringent inspection over foods coining into the port of Manchester. The Warrington Cor- poration strongly supported the application for the Order which, however, was opposed by the Man- chester Ship Canal Company on the ground that it would injure the trade to the port. The Order will I have to come up for confirmation in the House of Commons where it is likely that the company's ' opposition will be renewed. *s* After considering he circumstances of the recent inquest, the Infirmary Board of Management passed the following remarkable resolution and communicated the same to the Press : "That in the opinion of this Board, Dr. Brown acted with perfect judgment and in accordance with the rules of this institution, and for the welfare af the patient." Seeing that at the inquest, Dr. Brown himself admitted a slight error of judgment, which resulted indirectly in the patient's suicide, most people will be at a loss to understand how the Infirmary Board comes to the conclusion that " perfect judgment " was used I "for the welfare of the patient."</p>
        <p>4* * * Mr. Linaker, an active member of the Runcorn Rural District Council said on Tuesday that he thought the Cheshire County Education Committee was like a child which when it got a new toy at once proceeded to pull it to pieces. The expense incurred in this process has raised a considerable out-cry in the cowl!), and the Runcorn Council are seeking salvation in clause 26 of the Education Bill now before Parliament which permits local authorities to again assume con- trol of expenditure on education. They are confident that they can run the schools more economically than did the present administrators, and it will probably not necessitate any great effort on their part to suc- ceed.</p>
        <p>*s* Mr. Tobin, KO., who was Mr. Arthur Peacock's leading counsel, made a carious slip of the tongue in the &amp;airs() of his cross-examination of the chief witness. Seeking to show that the defendant had had consider- able experience in the building trade he asked Mr. Ffensball if he was not aware that Mr. Peacock had erected numerous buildings in Warrington including two " workhouses." Warrington people in the Court looked surprised. It was news to them to hear that the Guardians provided for the acconamo- dation of the poor on such a large scale, and Sir William Forwood, the presiding magistrate, asked: "How many workhouses have they in War- rington ?" Mr Tobin hastened to explain that he had meant "warehouses," adding, amid laughter, that if the jury could only see his handwriting they would' not be surprised at the mistake!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>ARRINGTONIAN IN SAN FRANCISCO.</head>
        <p>ARRINGTONIAN IN SAN FRANCISCO.</p>
        <p>Earthquake Experiences.</p>
        <p>The many friends of Mr. Thomas Emmerson, a native 1)1 Warrington, who went to San Francisco eighteen Years ago, will be pleased to hear that both he and his Wife are safe. His sister, Mrs. Owen, received a cable few days after the terrible calamity in San ncisco, and this week a letter has come to 'and giving full details. Mr. Emmerson's loss has tiet been great, as he resides in Oakland. but '4B wife's relatives have suffered considerably, as is extract from a sister's letter will show : suppose you think we are about swept away in that "readful fire, but I am very thankful to say that the 4tnall portion of proud, beautiful San Francisco, safe 11,:qa the fire, is the part we live in—Golden Gate alley. That fearful earthquake shook our flat terribly "4 smashed every bit of bric-a-brac 1 had and all my ,\4lluables, but I am thankful more than I can here ex- .;'jess that the dear children and I are alive and that ofi have a home at all. We have been having tilight shocks of earthquake every day since big one. The fire was horrible in the eri:ttreme. It Ilsted for four days and our tteleks were all stiff watching the flames spreading, and t e hot ashes covering us. Tom Emtnerson came over, rciln Oakland and took us all to his home, but how I ?et there, God only knows. We took with us a few :fundles that we could pack of clothes, as the soldiers truld not let us stop in the houses. They were carting ~WaY the dead and burying them in trenches. God rant I may never see such an awful sight again. very nervous as you may be sure and rtlye not got over the shock. We are back „ onr home hut have got no water or gas yet. •übeth rich and poor have to cook in the street We are I(i'lt wanting for anything but blankets and bedding and lit aye no doubt we shall be able to buy them after a tlfie while. Someone stole from the front door, the „IN night of the fire, my largest bundle, but things -4:ll,ttht have been worse.' j 4 ir• Emmerson thanks all his old friends for kind Jiniries and hopes to see them and" dear old Warring- " again.</p>
      </div>
