      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <p>naturally brings about increased competition for 1 . work in the towns. Small farmers are discouraged by having to pay proportionately larger rents than those who till big tracts, and this, of course, means , an influx to the towns and cities. The number : seeking a means of livelihood is increased, and wages as a consequence go down, Mr. Lester's suggestion of a uniform tax on all land, payable on its market value, is economically , sound. If this were done landowners could not afford to . allow their land to remain unproductive, while the , burden of tatation would also be more evenly distributed,</p>
        <p>THE SPANISH TRAGEDY.</p>
        <p>To-day, the civilized world stands aghast with horror fat the dastardly attempt lon the lives of King Alfonso and his newly-wedded Queen. A bomb, concealed ri a bouquet, was thrown at the Royal carriage When returning through crowds of wildly enthusiastic people, a few minutes after the ceremony. In a moment rejoicings were drowned by the roar of an explosion right in the midst of the Royal party, and there must have been a sickening feeling of horror and dread in the breasts of all around till it was seen that Alfonso and Ena—the youthful King ani Queen—were miraculously spared. A special Providence must have been watching over the Royal pair for one of the horses drawing their coach, and nine or ten people in the immediate vicinity, were killed outright. more cruel and senseless outrage could hardly be imagined. This boy and girl pair cannot have an enemy in the world, and the anarchy which would des, o wantonly will be execrated througout the civilized fi°he• The plot which failed—though it brought ~. i • 8124 e- d Yin its train—is said to have been hatched on; l'°.ridon. If so, is it not time the Government this country re-considered its attitude to the na:telchhist aliens who claim aright of asylum here? cruel outrages are the real outcome of their deliberations, the powers that be must take stern measures to stamp kait arid not harbour the obnoxious and insane anarchist. It is, we believe, a source of THE PEACOCK satisfaction to roost War- CASE. rington people to learn that the punishment fitted the the crime in respect to the charge of corrupt practice which Councillor Arthur Peacock was round guilty of at Liverpool Quarter Sessions on Tuesday. A fine of £lOO and seven years disquali- fication for holding any public office was none too severe under the circumstances. We hardly need emphasize once again the necessity for absolute trustworthiness in our public men—and especially those elected to serve the ratepayers—for probably one holds a contrary view. Fortunately—and lb is one of the things this country is justifiably Proud of—such a case as the one under notice is very rare. Political corruption, municipal corruption, and corruption in the judiciary are iings which,we are told abound in other countries. "...here is ample evidence in support of the asser- tion that bribery is looked upon as a disagreeable hut quite unavoidable formality, but we can latter ourselves that quite is niflerent standard of. morality in public affairs prevails in this country. The man who would seek pecuniary Personal profit while ostensibly serving the rate- Payers forfeits for ever the goodwill of his fellows laci renders himself liable to penalties which the slaw, jealous of public probity. rigorously inflicts. !Ire have no desire to harp on Mr. Peacock's case, heYond saying that it seems indicative of the canker which has been eating into the heart of ,Ivarrington municipal life for many years past. There have been revelations and revolutions in recent times, but it is hoped that the body cor- porate is by now purged of its impurities, and that the dawn of a cleaner, heall hier municipal era is at</p>
        <p>OUB Ns/BILITY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>Wanted by a respectable girl, her passage to New York; willing to take care of children and a good sailor.</p>
        <p>* * * In view of the exploitation of our district by daily contemporaries with tramcar ticket schemes—to use the phrasing of a Manchester paper--we are thinking of offering a row of houses, a motor car, and a copy of the" Examiner " to our own readers. Keep your seats—er, tram tickets ! * * * The text of the sermon at the Mortlake Congrega- tional Church last Sunday was : " Lay not up for yourselves treasures upon earth, where moth and rust doth corrupt, and where thieves break through and steal." While the sermon was being preached by the Rev. William Jones, 11. A., the minister's vestry was entered and his new overcoat was stolen.</p>
        <p>* * The fact that the young King of Spain went " travel-stained "into the presence of his bride's mother straight from a motor ride would never have tolerated in Queen Victoria's time. Her late Majesty was rigid in requiring riders to change before enter- ing her presence. However, motoring has overturned many rules of etiquette.</p>
        <p>*s* The workers of the Warrington Photographic Society deserve medals for their remarkalae energy. The exhibition which has been such a great success was brought to a close last Friday evening and so rapidly were the photographs taken down from the walls and dispatched, that the Art Gallery was ready for ordinary use at three o'clock on Saturday afternoon. *s*</p>
        <p>The Chester Union Education Committee have been agitated by the momentous question of school girls plaiting their hair. The mistress of the bole Girls' School recently sent three children home because hey went to school with their hair done in that way. Mr. T. Woollam stated that the parents had been pre- viously warned, and it was a standing rule that the children should not go to school with their hair in plaits.</p>
        <p>* * Sankey-street is being covered with tarred macadam. This is merely a sort of temporary disguise or summer suit. The actual re-paving cannot be done until the money has been borrowed—at least so we are told. Anyhow the new surface will be an improvement on the old. A cyclist who tried it before it was set says it is much softer to fall on. He needs a new suit of clothes in consequence, but that is a detail which the Corporation will perhaps attend to.</p>
        <p>* * * The speeches of counsel were a notable feature of the trial of a Warrington Councillor at Liverpool Sessions this week. They afforded an interesting con- trast in styles. While Mr. Rigby Swift, for the prosecution, unfolded his case in a calm yet forceful way, Mr. Tobin K.C., for the defence, made a fervent awl impassioned appeal to the jury, at times bordering on the pathetic. Each speech was really eloquent, and created a great impression in Court. * * *</p>
        <p>We hear that there is at last a prospect of some- thing definite being hone with regard to the widening of Warrington Bridge. The General Purposes Com- mittee recently appointed a sub-committee to specially report on the method of constructing bridges on the ferro-concrete principle. The sub-committee, we understand, are about to visit some of the bridges which have been constructed on these lines and the Chairman will probably give a full explanation at next Thursday's Council meeting.</p>
        <p>The consolidation of effort on behalf of theinfirmary Extension Scheme leaves no room for doubt that the necessary funds will soon be forthcoming. Not only are private subscribers being well backed up by the working men at some of the largest works, but the women are also putting a shoulder to the wheel. In another column, Mrs. Pickmere, (president of the Infirmary Ladies' Committee) appeals for assistance from the women and asks for the names of those who will help. We are sure there will be a good res- ponse.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, June 2, 1906.</p>
        <p>throughout the country about the cost of education, and he thought they would be acting wisely in placing the ' matter under local control. I Mr. Woodward said he would not go so far as Mr. Linaker in condemning the Committee for extravagance. They knew nothing except what was on the surface and until that extravagance was explained he would not say anything condemnatory or otherwise. The work ' was new and they had had difficult crices to face but he ; thought the resolution a wise one. Mr. Linaker said he did not say anything in a spirit lof vindictiveness but he thought the Committee had been very precipitate in their actions, and ought to have taken more time instead of rushing into expensive machinery as they had done. They reminded him very I much of a destructive child which when it got a new toy pulled it to pieces to see how it worked. I (Lau ght er.) IMr. Woodward : You cannot understand it until ; you have done so. (Laughter.) 1 Mr. Linaker: But you cannot put it together again. I The resolution was approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN MAN'S DEATH PRESUMED.</p>
        <p>In the Probate Division on Monday, application was made to Mr. Justice Bargrave Deane to presume the death in February, 1901, of Thomas Weston Pickering, a ship's engineer, formerly of Runcorn. The applica- tion was made on behalf of his mother. It was stated that there was a policy of insurance on his death for £2OO in the Mutual• Insurance Company. In February, 1901, he left Cape Town for South America, and was the second engineer on board the ship, which since that time had not been heard of, and had been posted at Lloyd's. The application for the presumption of death was granted. DRINK AND NEGLECT. - - -</p>
        <p>I At Runcorn. on Monday, Annie Elizabeth Wakefield I wife of John Wakefield, labourer, Millstone-yard, was I summoned for neglecting her three children. Inspector Rogers,'of the Royal Society for the Prevention of !Cruelty to Children, gave evidence that be had had the defendant's family under observation since the begin- ning of last December, and had visited the house many I times. The defendant with her husband and four child- ren, aged fifteen, fourteen,nine and seven, lived in a two roomed house. The place was very dirty. There was I only one bed for the whole family, and that was in a I dirty condition. He saw two of the children Edith I and Ernest, at Granville School. The girl was very dirty. The boy was lapt so bad. The mother was of drunken habits and lazy, and she never appeared to clean the house or wash the clothing. He had repeatedly warned the defendant. He had spoken to the husband, who said he could not help it. He was at work all day, ' and gave his wife the money. He bad often asked her to keep the house and the children cleaner.—The Chairman (Mr. Alfred Thomas) said the case disclosed an abominably disgraceful state of affairs. The husband was equally as bad as the wife, and it was fortunate for him that he was not included in the charge.—The defendant was sentenced to three months' hard labour</p>
        <p>RUNCORN FOOTBALL CLUB ANNUAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>IThe annual meeting of the members of the Runcorn I Football Club was held in Bethesda schoolroom, on Monday evening. Mr. E. Imison presided. There was a small attendance.—The Treasurer (Mr. C. Pownall) reported that at the beginning of the season 1903-6 there was nothing in hand in the general account, an they had to rely simply on the guarantee fund. They finished the season by having £l3 IGs, to credit in the bank, and an asset of £7 13s. 3d. due by the new,Liver- pool City Club, making a total balance of £2l 9s. 3d. I Be was sorry to report reductions in their receipts. The profit on drawing, as shown on the last balance I sheet, was £72 3s. 9d. This year it was £49 17s. 2d., a difference of £22 Gs. 7d. The committee set themselves I out to get £5O, and the result was very near, being : only a few shillings short of the estimate. The amount in subscriptions was less by £3B 165., the difference being due to the decline in their standard of play at the end of last season. Gate receipts were affected in the same way to a certain I extent, the reduction being £162 12s. 9d. They must not however, lose sight of the fact that last season they raised the sum of £B6 for loss of gate under the cup-tie rules. The balance account showed £lO7 Is. 7d. to the credit of the club after allowing £l7 10s. 4d. for depreciation of stock. In addition to this they had in the bank £62 I9s. 4d. in favour of the guarantee fund.— The Chairman stated that there was only one nomina- tion for the position of President, Mr. John Littler. He declared him duly elected. He also declared elected: Mr. C. Pownall, treasurer ; Mr. A. Abel, secretary; Mr. J. H. Littlemore, assistant secretary, and Messrs. T. C. Cooper. and E. Lunt, auditors. The voting for the other officials was by ballot, and resulted as follows :—J. Jolley, captain first team : J. Moran, vice-captain first team ; J. W. Hoxworth, captain second team •, W. She w, vice-captain second team ; committee, Messrs. Cawley, Ellison, Garner Garnett, Gregory, Grounds, Huxtable, Jolley, Jones, G. Moore and R. Moore.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>COUNTY COUNCIL EXPENDITURE.</head>
        <p>COUNTY COUNCIL EXPENDITURE.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN DISTRICT COUNCIL AND THE COST OF EDUCATION.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>Treatment of Vagrants.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Board of Guardians was held at Dutton on Tuesday when there were present :—Mr. A. R. Norman (presiding), Admiral !Jones Parry, Messrs. C. E. Linaker, S. Woodward, C. E. Linaker' jun., J. Douglas, S. Taylor, W. M. Morris, , T. Wright, C. Roylance, J. Spruce, H. Eyes, J. Whitley, W. Weir, J. Smith, J. \V. Tiraperley, J. Stokes' A. G. , Guest, G. E. Warburton G. Mounfield, P. Collins, W. Harrison, A. Broome, H. Mawdsley, T. Guest, G. Pilling, J. H. Davies, J. Fryer, A. Greenway, E. Bretherton, J. Whitlow, G. F. Ashton (clerk), W. E. Hough (assistant clerk), Cook and Pearson, (relieving officers), and J. ' Cannah. (workhouse master.) ADOPTED CHILDREN. The Farnham Union asked the Guardians to support I a petition they had presented to Parliament with a view Ito the better protection of infant life. The petition sought (1) to bring within the existing Act of 1897 all cases where one ctild only was taken in to be nursed' ! for reward ; (2) To do away with the limit of .£2O as ! regards children adopted on the payment of a sum of , money ; (3) To raise the age limit of children within 1 the Act to seven years ; (4) To enable the local ! authority to remove a child within the Act where the person having its charge, or the house in which it is kept is unfit or unsuitable ; (5) To render persons ' adopting children Opon pAyment of a sum down, and failing to notify such adoption liable to a fine or imprisonment—or both—as well as to forfeiture of the sum received ; (G) To require any DeISOLI having the charge of children within the Act to notify under penalty of fine and imprisonment any change of address to the Local Authorities of the district from which and to which such person moves, and to make it clear that the inspection of the children lasts until they attain the age limit, or are previously removed ,from the person having their charge. The resolution was referred to the Parliament Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <p>TREA.TMF 'T VAGRANT CHILDREN. The Clerk stated that the State Children's Associa- tion had forwardsd a copy of a Bill for the better pro- tection of children of vagrants. The Bill was first introduced in 1903, when it received a large measure of support from Boards of Guardians. Owing to the appointment of the Committee on Vagrancy it was deemed expedient to temporarily drop the measure. On the issue of the,Committee's report which recommended' that where a parent or guardian was dealt with as an habitual vagrant there should be power for the Court to remove the children from their custody, the Bill was re-introduced. Its objects were to save the children,to cut off the ever-increasing vagrancy at its source and to do so without extra financial demand upon individual Boards of Guardians for. whichlit was proposed that the charge should be a county and not a local one. Sup- port of the measure was asked. Mr. Stokes moved that the Board support the Bill. It was only right that the children of vagrant children should have a fair chance of life. If they were to get rid of vagrants they must get hold of the children. Mr. Woodward seconded. No man would go further than he would to pursue those parents who brought children into the world and then threw them on the public. The children of vagrant parents were Wing rained to become parasites on Soltioy and he wished to reven t that. (Hear, hear). Mr. Linaler suggested that they should add that vagrant parents should, after a certain number of con- victions, be sent the male to one colony and the female to another. Mr. Linaker's suggestion was approved and with this addition the resolution was approved. The report of the conference on the report of the Departmental Committee on Vagrancy was presented.At the conference objection was taken to tho police taking over the complete controllof vagrants,and it was decided to recommend that the vagrants should be admitted to the casual ward on a police order and that the police should have access to the wards. It was decided to support the resolution. COUCHES FOR INMATES. I Miss Bessie Dutton, on behalf of the Ladies' Visiting Committee, wrote enclosing tenders for couches for the infirmary wards. It was decided to empower the Committee to purchase four couches. The Ladies Visiting Committee reported that Miss B. Dutton had unanimously been re-elected president and Mrs. I'dilner had been re-appointed hon. secretary.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Rura I District Council wad held on Tuesday when there were present: —Mr. Pilling (presiding), Admiral Jones-Parry, I Messrs. T. Baxter, C. E. Linakor, S. Woodward, C. E. Linaker, jun., T. Wright, C. Roylance, J. Spruce, H. Eyes, J. Whitley, W. Weir, J. Smith, J. Stokes, A. G. Guest, G. E. Warburton, G. Mounfieid. W. Harrison, A. Broome, H. Mawdsley, T. Guest, G. Pilling, J. I-I. I Davies, J. Fryer, A. Greenway, E. Bretherton, J. Whitlow, G. F. Ashton (clerk). W. E. Hough (assistant I clerk), J. Swinton (surveyor), J. Farrington (inspector) I and Dr. Adams (medical officer). THE COST OF EDUCATION. The Clerk read a letter from the County Council in answer to a resolution from the District council. stat-</p>
        <p>ing that the Finance Committee had passed a resolution to the effect that whilst the. cost of education had</p>
