      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>WARRINGTON</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>Notice.</p>
        <p>_ The Warrington "Examiner" is rtyr published on Friday afternoon, price one penny, and is on sale at the various newsagents at about 4 o'clock. Should any difficulty be experienced in getting the paper the proprietors will undertake to deliver a copy at any address in Warrington by 5 o'clock on Friday after- noon without extra charge. Address all communica, tions to" Examiner Office," Bewsey-street Warrington Town Council Meeting Postponed. Owing to the Whitsuntide holidays the Council meeting fixed for Tuesday next has been postponed until Thursday next. Labour Bureau. During the week 30 persons 'have been registered making the total number 547. Fourteen have obtained work and there now on the register 152 names. Compensation for Warrington Licenses. Persons interested in the licenses of the Rope and Anchor Inn, Queen's Hotel and the Boars Head Inn, which were extinguished by the Compensation Authority, are called ,ipon to send in their claims for compensation to that body before the 23rd inst. Warrington Swimming Club. The number of entries this year for the Works Squadron Competition (Sir Gilbeit Greenall Challenge Shield) is very satisfactory, 14 teams having entered. The competing teams are as follows : —J. Crosfield and Sons, 2 teams ; P. Walker and Sons, 2 teams ; Post Office, 2 teams ; Rvlands and Co„ Ltd. ; Rich- mond and Co., Ltd. ; Fletcher, Russel and Co., Ltd. ; Sankey Wire Mills ; Monks, Hall and Co., Ltd.; Co-operative Stores ; Warrington Corporation ; Pear- son and Knowles, Ltd. The first round of the com- petition will be decided on Thursday, June 14th, when some exciting racing is anticipated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <p>Funeral of the Late Mr. D. MeLady. The funeral of the late Mr. Donald McLarty, whose death we announced last week took place at the Warrington Cemetery on Saturday afternoon. The deceased had been in the employ of Messrs: J. Fairclough and Sons for 43 years and a tribute of respect w as paid by the firm,who were represented at the funeral by Messrs. John,Richard and Henry Fairclough. In addition about 30 of the deceased's fellow employees walked to the graveside. The chief mourners present were Mr. and, Mrs. Gidinan and Mr. and Mrs. H. Clayton (daughters and sons-in-law). Mr. Mann (son- in-law) and Messrs. W. Humphreys and It Mann (grandchildren): Floral tributes were sent by the widow and children, Messrs. J. Fairclough and Sons, Mr. Henry Fairclough and the workmen employed by Messrs. Fairclough. "A Disreputable Theft."</p>
        <p>. - At Warrington Police Court, on Saturday, Peter Rowson, jun., 26, Bowes-street, and Mired France, I Dial Court, were charged with stealing and receiving six brass guides, weighing 1 cwt. 2qr. Gib., valued at :e7 75., the property of the Pearson and Knowles, I Coal and Iron Co., Ltd. Mr. F. G. Taylor, solicitor I (Messrs. Greenall and Co.), prosecuted, and stated that the guides were Attings used in an llin. hoop mill at the works On Sunday morning they were seen in their places on the mill by a man named James Whitehouse, but at six o'clock the following morning James Whitehouse missed them. On Tues- day Joseph Matthews, Lythgoes-lane, a metal dealer was seen by Rowson, who told him he had about 2cwt. of good brass for sale. Matthews was suspicious, and gave information to the police with the result that the men were arrested by Detective Meacock whilst in the act of weighing the brass. France I remarked "It is a fair catch, but I would rather you got it than Clough. I suppose it will be something for you." These thefts of brass from the Pearson and Knowles Works were constant, and caused a great deal of inconvenience and in that case a number of men were temporarily thrown out of work, whilst the guides were being replaced. He was instructed to press the case, in order that it /nigh+ be a warning to others.—The Bench who described the theft as a particularly disreputable one sentenced France who had a bad record to six months' imprisonment, with hard labour, and Rowson to three months, also with hard labour.</p>
        <p>Beautiful Warrington Society. A meeting of the Tree Planting and Open Spaces Committee was held at the Old Academy on Monday evening last, Dr. Markel in the chair. The Secretary of the Society, Mr. Fairhurst, reported inter alla that IMr. Edwin Woods had repaired the fencing of the i 1 Knutsford-road plantation, while for the one n Hazel- street Messrs. Rylands Bros. bad undertaken to do the sanw. The residents in Salisbury-street were arrang- ing to protect the trees they had planted there and similar arrangements were being made for those near Fairfield Hall. A prize competition was to be held for the gardens in Wakefield-street. With regard to the Juvenile Branch he was able to announce that seeds had already been distributed and 2,500 plants would be given out at an early date. As many as 500 had been promised by Sir Peter Walker, and a large number by Mr. F. Monks. These, together with 500 which had been ordered, would enable them to make an immediate start. Mr. Darlington had in ' accordance with the resolution of the Committee got ; tenders, for the supply of window boxes, &amp;c., and these he had to save time submitted to the General Corn- mitten who had accepted the lowest. The rAadt of this award was embodied in a leaflet, the proof of ; which he now produced.—lt was resolved that copies of this leaflet be issued to the Ward Secretaries, to be ! by them distributed through their committees. The question was now considered of planting the piece of ; land at the corner between Helsby-street and ; Manchester-road, which has been offered for the pur-! pese. The general lines were agreed upon, and the : execution of the idea was left to the Ward Committee. ! money being voted for the purpose. Mr. Fairhurst ; further reported the artangements made for the ; Flower Show. This has now been definitely fixed for ; Ist September, an earlier date having been found i impracticable, and an effort will be made to obtain the use of the Public Park for the purpose. Among other business the most important was the appoint- ment of a sub-committee consisting of the chairman with Rev. C. H. Cook, Messrs. Fulton Smith, Ryan and the secretary to consider a moderate scheme of street planting to be laid before the Street Improvement Committee of the Corporation.—A meeting of the Show Committee was held on Thursday evening when it was decided to hold the show on Saturday,September Ist. The object of the show is to encourage the cultivation of plants and flowers by the members of the Society thereby beautifying their surroundings. The committee hope that everyone interested will assist; them to make this their first show,a success. Schedules ! are being prepared and will be ready for distribution ' in a fortnight. These can be obtained from any mem- ber of the committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
