        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <p>hildren's Summer Camp. The directors, officials, and employees of the Rich- mond Gas Stove and Meter Co., Ltd., have very kindly presented the Poor Children's Camp Commqtee with a hut which is to be called the " Richmond hut." Music in the Park. The League of the Cross Band will occupy the Band- stand inithe Park on Sunday next and play the follow- ing programme under the conductorship of Mr. T. Hynes. Hymn, "0 Salutaris," arr. by T. Hynes ; march " Enchanter," J. H. White ; selection, " Satanella," M. W. Balfe, selection, " Falstaff," Halle; concert overture, " Don Quixote," H. Round ; selection, "Recollections of Flotoro," Round ; " God save the King.," UNDER THE CLOCK for Good Booxs.—Adot. Local Wedding. The marriage was solemnized at St. Luke's Church, Liverpool-road, on Thursday morning of Miss Hannah! M^ody, fifth daughter of Mr. William Moody, 287, ; . Liverpool-road, and Ur. George Perdue, eldest son of 'Mr. Robert Perdue, Higher Angel Inn, Buttermarket- street. The Rev. H. R. Wild officiated. The bride, who was given away by Yr. J. P. Jackson, was attended by two bridesmaids, Miss Mary MoOdy (sister of the bride) and. Miss Dorothy Perdue (sister of the bridegroom). The duties of best man wers discharged by Mr. Joseph Perdue (brother of the bridegroom.) Later in the day the newly-married couple left for London where the honeymoon is being spent. . Presentation to Mr. Ernest Haigh. A few weeks ago we announced that Mr. Ernest Haigh of Warrington had obtained an appointment at Wimbledon, London under the Education Board there. Last week the members of the Wesleyan Bible Class, Padgate, held a social and took that opportunity of presenting Mr. Haigh with a neat dressing case at the same time wishing him a prosperous career Speeches were made by Mr. G. Plinston,Mr. Seed,Nir. Mason and others. Mr. Haigh who was a former secretary of the Society bore testimony not only in his own case, but also that of others, of the valuable work the class was doing in that neighbourhood. BABY CARRIAGES. Latest patterns.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. Forthcoming Horse Parade. The sixth annual horse parade will be held on Saturday, June 9th, and arrangements are now com- plete for a successful show. Entries close to-day (Saturday) arid already a splendid list has been received. The special prize given by Mr. Peter Davies for the best heavy horse has been supplied by Mr. G. D. Southerden (late Blackhurst and Son) and will be on view in the window of Messrs. Geo. Venn and Son,llo,Bridge-street. All turnouts must be on the ground at Arpley at 11.45 a.m. on the day of parade and judging will commence at 12 o'clock. Any competitor not in his place at that time will be dis- qualified. The procession will start at 2 o'clock and non-competitors wishing to join in it must be in their places in Slutchers-lane at 1.30. LADIES' BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOES, UNDER THE CLOCK. BRIDGE-STREET.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <p>Presentation at Whyman's Foundry. There was a pleasing ceremony at the works of Whyman's Foundry Co., Ltd., on Thurschiy afternoon when Mr. W. Mitchell, foreman of the moulding department, who is shortly to be married, was pre- sented by his fellow-employees with agold albeit and pendant. Mr. E. J. Williams, store keeper, opened the proceedings with a few well-chosen remarks and called upon Mr. Whyman to make the presentation. Mr. Whyman spoke very highly of the services rendered by Mr. Mitchell. He pointed out that a foreman occupied the position of a "go between" the • masters and men, and from his experience he \ theught Mr. Mitchell had fulfilled the duties very creditably. Any little differences which had occurred had always been fairly represented front both points of view. He had pleasure in handing Mr. Mitchell the chain and trusted that he would have a very long and happy flurried life.—The pendant is inscribed on one side : "W. M., June 4th, 1900," and on the reverse: "Presented by the employees of Whyrrian's Foundry Cu., Ltd.'.'—Mr. Mitchell briefly thanked the employees for their kindness and particu- larly for the good feeling which their gift expressed. —The proceedings closed with cheers for Mr. Mitchell. J, R. WOOD, Piano Dealer and Tuner, 47, Old London-road., Stockton Heath.—Advt. Cheshire County Junior Scholarships. Mr. R. P. Ward, Director of Education for the County Council of Cheshire has issued a report on the examination for county junior scholarships. The total number of candidates examined was 798. The sixty scholarships offered are allocated to the various boroughs, the urban district of Wallasey, and the respective county administrative areas, in proportion to the total number of children in average attendance at the elementary schools in such boroughs .or districts for the year 1905. It will be found that the Scholarships are spread over 46 schools, a larger number than usual, and a pleasing feature is the good work that has been done by candi- dates from rural schools and small schools. In many of these schools the work had been reported on from I time to time by the County Inspectors of Schoils as !being good, and the instruction given in an intelligent Imanner. The winners of scholarships in this district are Arthur Ackerley, William. F. Hammon, and Wilhehnina Corvil (Altrinchain), Sarah M. Goodier I(Lyrnm), Thomas Clarke, Andrew H. Matthews, Henry I V. Rowe (Runcorn Rural), Dora Gandy, and Edgar T. Lawton (Runcorn Urban). Stx-Gursra CYCLES. Excellent value. Call and inspect.—J. S. GARIAND,SI to 55, Bridge-Bt.—Advt, Good Tempiary In Lancashire. The quarterly session of the South-West Lancashire District Lodge was held on Saturday in the Temper- ance Institute, Southport, a very large assembly being presided over by the District Chief Templar (Mr. R. D. Petterson, of Liverpool). Hearty congratulations were tendered to Mrs. Walker upon her recent election : to the high officer of Grand Vice-Templar of England. i The ofticers'ireport dealt with important matters, such as mission work, a missioner being now at work in the district for a period of six weeks, it being intended to open up ground at present unoccupied. The District Secretary (Mr. Morris Jones) gave the , numerical position of the adult department, I and Mr. J. T. Oninto, D.S.J.W., that of the juvenile I depaitment. A national bazaar is to be held next I year, a large committee of ladies being appointed tol I help that project., Mr. G. S. Schofield,D.E.S. (Bootle),1 gave details of the arrangements for electoral work. I Considerable discussion took place on financial ques- tions, introduced by the treasurer (Mr. G. B. Walker). i The District Councillor (Mr. J. Oliver), on behalf of I ; the District Executive, gave particulars of many plans ; for carrying on aggressive work during the summer, I I there having been arranged a large number of open- air meetings in all parts of South-West Lancashire, The next session is to be held at St. Helens on the Ist II September. Crosfleids' Recreation Club. The draughts section of this club concluded its Iseason on Tuesday evening la 4 with an exhibition of simultaneous play by the captain, Mr. W. Tomkinson, • who succeeded in winning 10 and drawing 2 out of 12 Igames. At the ae of play Dr. Markel presented the i prizes to The winners in the' recant tourney. In a I happy speech he congratulated the members on the I continued progress of the club, in whose operations he took the keenest interest, not only becaifse of the . high value he attached to the game itself as develop- ing both intelligence and character but because of the opportunities it gave for meeting one another on the Icenunon ground of good fellowship. He also con- veyed to Mr. Tonakinson on behalf of those present their thanks not merely for his present exhibition but Ifor his unselfish devotion to the interests of the club. Mr. Tomkinson in acknowledging mentioned that during the season just ending their second divison team had not lost a single game and were in conse- quence easily firat in th a league competition. The first division team had been almost equally successful having been beaten by the Warrington club alone. He could remember tke time when other clubs thought they had "a soft thing" when they met Crosfields'. Now they were top of the works' teams of the district. The following were the prize-Winners in order :—J. S. Fleming, W. Heywood, J. Inman, W.Fynan,H.G-ardner, J. Rime, F. Dendy,_T. Ward.</p>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S CREAM CRACKERS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="3" unit="words">
