        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <p>The Baths Committee and the Scholars. The Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee have refused the application, of the Education Committee for permission for theybiths-keeper to give instruction in swimming to children attending the elementary schools.</p>
        <p>Testimonial to the Rev. H. Dunnico. It has been decided to make a public testimonial to the Rev. 11. Dunuico who closes his ministry at Golborne-street on June 24th. A representative elm- mittee has been appointed with Mr. David Grundy as chairman, Jr. Joseph Elwell and fr. A. P. Freeman as joint hon. secretaries and Mr. Edmund Osborne as treasurer.</p>
        <p>Father and his Son's Bankruptcy. There was an unusual incident at the Bankruptcy Court this (Friday) morning when Edwin Davies, 22, Edge-lane, Golborne, lately residing at 86, flaydock- st., Earlestown, and formerly residing and carrying on business as a wheelwright and blacksmith at Golborne appeared for .his public examination. The liabilities were estimated at .02 Bs. sd. and after allowing for preferential creditors the assets were expected to rank at 10s. 3d., leaving a deficiency of £9l l Bs. 2d.— Debtors father attended the proceedings and soundly rated his son for having come to such a position, alleging that he had incurred too much expense at 'home in giving tea parties and such functions.— This defendant denied—The examination was closed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>ROYAL WEDDING.</head>
        <p>THE ROYAL WEDDING.</p>
        <p>BOMB THROWN AT KING ALFONSO AND HIS BRIDE.</p>
        <p>A Miraculous Escape.</p>
        <p>NINE LIVES .LOST.</p>
        <p>14 the newly-wed Royal couple were driving from , the Church of San Geronimo at Madrid, yesterday, through throngs of loudly cheering people, and were passing down the imposingly decorated Calle Mayor, bomb was thrown hidden in a bouquet. It fell quite close to the Royal carriage, which, in fact, was badly damaged, while two of the horses were killed outright. Fortunately the Royal couple escaped sr7athless,but in a‘ nerve-shaken condition and in tears. Nine people were killed, including two women spectators. The police bugler had his head blown from his body. Many arrests were made, but the actual culprit is still at large. Curiously enough yesterday was the anniversary of</p>
        <p>the attempt on King Alfonso's life during his visit to Paris last year. A bomb was then thrown at the carriage in which his Majesty was driving:through the Paris streets in company with President Loubet. after leaving the Opera House. On that occasion also a horse, was killed, but neither King Alfonso nor President Loubet sustained any injury.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>VACANCY IN ORFORD WARD.</head>
        <p>THE VACANCY IN ORFORD WARD.</p>
        <p>Non-Trading Candidate in. the Field.</p>
        <p>The conviction of Councillor A. Peacock under the Corrupt Practices Act, renders vacant the seat he has • occupied on the Council as a representative of Orford Ward. In the ordinary course, Councillor Peacock would have retired for re-election next November as</p>
        <p>he has already served mare than two of the three years for which he was elected. Whoever succeeds him, therefore, will again havo to coma before the electors six months hence. He was elected to the Council in 1897.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <p>On Wednesday morning Mr. Jame; Dodds waited upon Mr. Thomas Mill, butcher, Winwick-road, to invito him to stand as a Non-trading candidate. Mr. Hill was inclined to favourably consider the request, but subsequently, we learn, he declined to stand. A candidate has been, however, found by the Non- Traders in Mr. Elijah Cunuington, 32 Longford-st., who has accepted the invitation t) stand in their bebalf. A number of years ago, we believe, Mr. Cunnington opposed the late Mr. J. Shaw Green in a mnaicipal contest but was defeated. I It is expected that the vacancy will be declared immediately, and the election take place in about a fortnight. Up to pow there are no politicll nominees mentioned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</head>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</p>
        <p>NorrcE TO CORRESPONDENTS. Authors of letters intended for publication must La all cases send their names and addresses to the Editor as a guarantee of good faith.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>– Infirmary Extension—Appeal to Women.</head>
        <p>- Infirmary Extension—Appeal to Women.</p>
        <p>Sir,—lt is most praiseworthy and noble, the way in which the working men are helping, towards the Infirmary Extension Pond. It occurs toame that a good deal of help could beF given, by the women of all classes of A'arring,toa and the surrounding district, and I shall he glad if any of - those who can assist will send me their names. I will then call a meeting for the carrying out of this idea. Thanking you for pahlishing this appeal.—Yours MARGARET PICEMERE. President of the Infirmary Ladies' Committee. Thelwell.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>Peacock Case</head>
        <p>The Peacock Case</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln the evidence of Mr. Arthur Peacock, the defendant, he is reported to have stated that both he and Mr. Louis flenshalt belonged to the" purity side in the Non-trading agitation." r need hardly say that this is directly contrary to the facts, as the purity cause, frbm its origin and throughout, has had no stronger opponent in and out of the Council than Mr. Arthur reacock. I obsdtve also that the Town Clerk stated in his evid- ence that for services rendered as a Trading Inquiry r Commissioner, the Council passed art unanimous vote of thanks to Mr. Arthur Peacock. I find on referring to your report of the Council proceedings at which the vote of thanks was passed, that, although it was carried, six voted against it. As the majority for the vote was also six, it is clear that only twelve (or one third of the members of the Council) voted for it !—I an etc., JAMES .DODDS, IChairman, Warrington Siunicipal Association, June Ist</p>
        <p>Sunday Observance.---A Challenge, Sir,—Anyone reading the admirable and lucikl. letter by" lona' in your last issue, cannot help noticing the many truthful and wise remarks he makes. Not least is the one referring to the few points which British professors of Christianity have in common. The fact is, there is only one point on which thy all agree, and although it may be news to many who are sincere seekers after righteousness, nevertheless that one point has no Biblical foundation. I refer to Sunday Observance.</p>
        <p>To stimulate the study of the Scriptures, I will give fifty pounds (450) to any person who can give:— (1.) Any Bible command to keep holy the first dny of the week (Sunday). (2.) Any Bible Text calling the first day of the week the Sabbath.</p>
        <p>(3.) Any Bible text prohibiting the doing of this, or that, on tho first day of the week (Sunday). (4.) Any Bible text authorising the change of the Sabbath froin the seventh day, to the first day of the week (Sunday). (5.) Any Bible text where a blessing is pronounced on anyone keeping, and a curse on anyone working on the first day of the week (Sunday.) (6. Any Bible text calling the first day of the week (Sunday) the Lord's day.—Yours truly. JOHN F. SMITH, (A Sabbath (not Sunday) keeping Christian.) Prestatyn, May 29th, 190 G.</p>
        <p>Clerical Changes. The Rev. Father J. H. Roche, of St. Vincent Church, Knutsford, will shortly take over new duties at St. • Mary's Church, Latchford. The Rev. Father Marrs, Hyde, has been appointed assistant priest at the same church.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>SHERRY, REVIVAL.</head>
        <p>THE SHERRY, REVIVAL.</p>
        <p>An interesting feature at the present time, in view of the wedding of the English Princess with the King of Spain, is the re-introduction of a very fine old Amoroso Sherry by Messrs. Hedges and Butler, of Regent-street, London, W. This old-established firm, who are wine merchants to his Majesty the King of Spain, are retain- ing the appropriate name of the brand—La Novia (the bride)—and are uniting in the label the English and Spanish</p>
      </div>
