      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>TAXATION OF LAND VALUES. .</head>
        <p>TAXATION OF LAND VALUES. .</p>
        <p>A Remedy for Social Evils.</p>
        <p>Under the auspices of the English League for the Vocation of land values a lecture was delivered in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street, on Friday evening by Mr. W. R. Lester. Owing to the inclementary of the weather there was only a small attendance. Mr. W. H. Sherbnrn presided. Mr. LESTER said that when any thoughtful man looked round to-day and saw the enormous progress in pro- duction which had been made during the past century he must be shocked at the fact that the great mass of the working people of the country still lived in a state of hand to mouth. With them it was 'one continual struggle to keep the wolf from the door and that being so every man must realize that deeply below the surface there was something wrong. Men like Sir Henry Campbell-Bannerman, Mr, Chamberlain, Mr. Rowntree, of York, and Mr. Booth, of London, were all agreed that there were 12.000,000 people in this country to-day on the verge of want. Want of employment pushed them down to the point of destitution. There was I something vitally wrong. and it was their business to I find out what it was. After a somewhat long study of the question he had come to the conclusion that all soil troubles were to be traced to the fact that we had violated the most elementary and vital right of every human being born, into the world to use the earth. Every infant born had an equal right with any other infant to live, and it was not within the bounds of possibility to support life without access to land. It was a very bad thing that a few men should control the land. Most people did not connect the land monopoly with the problem of unemployment, bad housing condi- tions, cut-throat competition, the question of the slums and wages, but he assured them that this waa the chief cause. In the solution of these social evils many people turned to socialism whilst others were repelled from socialism because of its cast iron methods and because of its destruction of individual activity. There was only one way in which they could get employment and that was br haying free access to the land. It also had a great 10e-ing upon the wages question. Recently in South Af "'Alt an effort had been made to reduce the wages of the Kaffir with the result that they declined , to work and went back to the land, whereas in England the worker had not the opportunity of going back to the land. To show this the lecturer proceeded to instance the dispute between Lard l'enrhyn and the workers in his quarries. They ought to devise some means of driving Lord Penrhyn to make use of those natural sources of wealth and thus allow 3,000 quarrymen to go back to their work. If they could do that. lite considered they would have gone far to solve that whole question. The tendency to wihtbold land was, in his opinion, largely responsible for unemployment. (Hear, hear.) The monopoly of land had three consequences. • It depressed wages, raised rent, and intensified the evil of the housing problem. If they could arrange to drive all the land into use, they would have an enormous demand for it, trade would boom, end the unemployed question would be solved. The whole power of the employer— the whole undue power of capital, he shonldsay—rested on the existence of the unemployed classes, and if they I coad do away with unemployment they would do away with any power capital had to sweat labour. How were they going to tackle the question? Supposing municipalities had the power to go to every landowner and assess the value of his land on what it would fetch by aAtion in an open market., what would be the consequences? They would have the landowners running after the tenants instead of the tenants running after the landowners as they were to-day. The obvious remedy was to have a ten per cent, tax on all land. The resnits of this would be (1) competition amongst land- owners to let their land would increase and rents would go down : (2) competition for employment would go down and wages would go up; and (3) rates would be lowered because they would be bringing under contri- bution all the existing sites that did not contribute at present. Freedom would accomplish everything and the taxation of land values was on the lines of freedom , and made for tEe solution of all those perplexing problems now before the country. (Applause.) The lecturer was heartily thanked at the close of his address.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>WARRINGTON INCORRIGIBLE.</head>
        <p>A WARRINGTON INCORRIGIBLE.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Court, on Saturday, James Quigley, of no settled address, was charged with having been drunk and disorderly in Mersey-street, on Friday, and also with assaulting P.C. Kenwright. Kenwright stated that he was off duty in plain clothes in Mersey street on Friday afternoon, when prisoner, who was drunk, was making use of bad language. Witness told him that he was a policeman, and asked him to go home but prisoner refused, and made a nick at witness. They closed, and on the ground. prisoner kicked witness on the head with his clog. Supt. Monaghan stated that prisoner was incorrigible. He was the worst chaiacter he (the Superintendent) had ever seen. He had bc3n before the Court 26 times, including six times for assaulting the police. On the last occasion he WEI 9 sent to gaol for six months. He was a terror to the neigh- bourhood. Prisoner was sent to gaol for three months with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>ANOTHER TURBINE RECORD.</head>
        <p>ANOTHER TURBINE RECORD.</p>
        <p>The Allan line turbine steamer Virginian, which left Moville at 2 p.m. on Friday last, 25th inst., we.; reported at Cape Race at 9 a.m. on Tuesday, thus beat- ing the previous record made by the Victorian by about eleven hours. The quick time in which the Atlantic was crossed from land to land, viz., 3 days 17 hours, is a significant feature of the Virginian's record and of the Canadian route.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>WARRINGTON</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>Pianos in Cheshire Schools. In order to brighten the lives of children in Cheshire schools, the County Counci have suggested that in every school there shall be a piano. In all the County Council schools they have decided that pianos shall 13 provided at the expense of the rates, and in voluntary schools they have agree i to pay one half the cost of pianos. The total cost will be about £l,OOO. Licensed Victuallers at Plymouth.</p>
        <p>The 31th annual conference of the Licensed Victuallers' National Defence League of England and Wales was held a few d.tys a,.0 at Plymouth. The Warrington L;cense,i Victuallers' Association was represented by M,. Alderman Evans (president and vice-president of the district), Mr. Thomas Walsh (secretary), aid Mr. Perdue.</p>
        <p>, PREMIER CYCLES, 7,8, 10, 12 and 14 guineas Cash or 18 monthly payments. Sole agent, J. S. GARLAND, 51, 53 and 35, Bridge-st.—Advt. General Booth to Visit Warrington. Arrangements have been made for Generti Baoth to make a summer tour through Cumberland, West- morland. Cheshire, and Lancashire. He will start from Keswick, alter a meeting, on August Bth, and will be busy until the 17th, when In will concluie his tour at Warrington, where he will deliver an address in the evening.</p>
        <p>Warrington P.S.A. The weekly meeting of the Warrington Society was held in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street, on Sunday, and was well attended. Mr. J. Callon pre- sided, and gave a short address on " The Christian Athlete." Mr. Ffe9ry Taylor (Police C nut Missionary) spoke on " Christian Service." Mr. P. Dandy rendered a solo, Mr. A. Pownall was the acc)mpanist. Mr. R. Nelson and Mr. W. Light also took part in the service.</p>
        <p>Old Age Pensions Scheme at Culcheth. The Culcheth Oddfallows, having a large surplus in hand, have decided, by 80 votes to 40, to institute a pension scheme for their aged members. The branch is worth £.5 1,55. per member over and above any possible expense on lruefit and funeral expenditure, and a proposal for the payment of an additional 2s. per annum by the members to meet the expenses of the old age pensions experiment was rejected, a com- mittee being appornted to formulate a scheme.</p>
        <p>LAWN TENNI% CROQUET, and other outdoor sports.—J. S. GARLAND, 51,Bridge-st.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Fire Brigade Demonstration. The Lancashire Fire Brigade Friendly Society held its annual demonstration at Lancaster on Saturday, visiting the county town for the second time, No fewer than ninety-three brigades were represented.— The annual meeting was held under the presidency of the Mayor of Lancaster (Councillor Sattherwaite), who subsequently entertained the officers, judges, and officials to lunch. The parade through the town of all the brigades was witnessed by large crowds of people, and at the Giant Axe Field, where the competitions took place, the brigades were inspected by Sir Henry F. Hibbert, of Chorley. In the hand huse-cartj dry drill (31 entries), the Warrington Brigade tied with Darwen for third place, but the judges favoured Darwen for general smartness. The first prize in the firemen's dressing race was won by J. Whitby of Warrington. STILL LEADING, the "HERALD" KITCHEN RANGES and " PEVERIL " FIRE GRATES Sole Agent.—J. S. GA RLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge-st., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Warrington Homing Club's Race The old birds' race frorn Bath in connection with the Warrington Workingmen's Club took place on Saturday. Thirty members sent birds and the con- voyer,Mr. S Taylor,liberated3l7 birds at 12.56,a south- wind west prevailing. Considering that heavy- clouds hampered the homers on their journey, good times were recorded. Mr. Maikin won Mr. Reid's special prize of 103. 6d. The following velocities were returned : —Malkin, 1,143,; Stringfellow, 1,133; Roth- well, 1,119; Perry and Scott, 1,101; Hatton and East- wood, 1,094; Rothwell, 1.086; Clark Bros., 1,079; Hatton and Eastwood, 1,075; Clark Bros., 1,072 ; Bracegirdle, 1,071; Carpenter, 1,071; Crank Bros., 1,064; Hughes, 1,061; Pomfrett and Belton, 1,059; Craven, 1,040; Burn's, 1,046; Bracegirdle, 1,041 ; Stringfellow, 1,040; Healey, 1,039; Healey, 1,037 ; Ray, 1,029; Hatch, 1,020; Jackson and Goulden, 1,012; Izimblett and Brook, 1,000; Dixon, 991; Hatton, 904: Caddock, 983; Laycock and Blackstiaw. 980; Boden, 973; Irlam, 972; Wright and Son, 971 ; Clare, 968; Mather, 965; Mason and Williams, 961 ; and Williams, 946. Appleton Rifle Club</p>
        <p>- - The first monthly competition for the present season took place at the Range last Saturday, there being only a small number of members present, which was probably largely owing to the showery weather which prevailed. Entries were booked in the medal competition, members' stakes, and open sweep-stakes. The metal competition, consists of ten shots at one hundred yards, three inch bull, fixed target : and ten shots at a disappearing target ; fired with a service rifle ; the highest possible score being fifty in each , stage. The winner in this comptition was Mr. F. J. Grocott, with a score of twenty-one points at a fixed I target, and twenty at the disappearing target. The final stage of this competition will take place on the 29th of September, when the Nailonal Rifle Associa- tion's bronze medal will be competed for. The! members' stakes consists of seven shots, at one hundred yards, six inch bull, any rifle ; the highest possible score being twenty-eight. This was won by Mr. A. Down, with a score of twenty-five. The con- ditions; of] the open sweep-stakes, are five shots at one hundred yards, three inch bull, any rifle; the highest possible score being twenty points. The successful competitor in this class, was Mr. F. J. Grecott, with a score of twelve points. Competitions I will be hold at the Range on the following Saturdays, lbetween the hours of 3 and 5 p.m., viz. : —3oth June, 128th July, 25th August, 29th September, being the last Saturday in each month. LAWN MOWERS, Garden Tools, 8;:e. Splendid selection.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge-st.—Advt. Spiritualism. The quarterly meeting of the Liverpopl District Spiritualist Lyceum Union was held in the hall of the local society on Saturday, Mr. Stout (the local presi- dent) was asked ta preside in the absence of Mr. Belshaw, of Wigan. After the transaction of busi- ness tea was partaken of, provided by the Warrington friends, and afterwards a public meeting was held, when addresses were del;vered by several of the delegates. On Sunday the Lyceum sessions were visited by Mr. Belshaw (president), Mr. Keeling (of Liverpool, secretary), Mr. R. A. Owen (of Bootle, district visitor), Mrs. Kay (of Southport), and Mr. Belshaw, jun. (of Wigan). Addresses interesting to both children and adults were given both morning and afternoon, and when called upon several of the local Lyceumists gave recitations. In the evening Mr. Belshaw, sen., presided at the usual service. Lyceum songs were sung, and addresses were given by Mr. R. A. Owen and Mr. Belshaw, jun., followed by clairvoy- ance by Mr. Evans, of Liverpool. Mr. Herbert Foster sang "Cathedral voices," with taLgte. On Monday evening the usual meeting was conducted by Mr. C. Llewellyn, who gave a short address on "The Resur- ection,' afterwards giving clairvoyance. PUBLIC BENEFIT, UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. School Attendance Officers' Meeting. The quarterly meeting of the Warrington and District Branch of the School Attendance Officers' National Association was held at the Education Office, Sankey-street, on Saturday. The Vice-president, Mr. Thomas Bromilow (St. Helens) presided, and there were also present: Messrs. William Mercer (St. He] ens),Ernest Barlow and Cooper(Farnworth,Bolton). William Rutter, T. W. Holbrook and William Farrar (secretary), Warrington. Mr. Earnest Barlow gave an account of the Easter National Conference, held at Newcastle, and read a paper, entitled " Our Union." On the proposition of Mr. W. Rutter a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to Mr. Barlow for his instructive paper. On the proposition of Mr. Rutter, seconded by. Mr. Mercer, it was resolved : "That, in the opinion of this meeting, a uniform system of returns is desirable so that real and proper comparisons can be made." Mr. William Farrar *as instructed to notify the same to the General Secretary of the Associa- tion, Mr. E. it. Cook (London). It was also resolved: "That it would aid local authorities in their efforts to get children into school, especially on Mondayg, and protect those children from the immoral conversation and influence of those who habitually assemble at the doors of pawnshops, if it were made an offence against the Pawnbrokers' Act, 1872, for a pawnbroker to take into pledge any article from any claild under 14 years of age." The Secretary was instructed to write to Mr. J, Moore Murray, thanking him for the use of the room for the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>Examiner, Soturday, June 2, 1906.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Soturday, June 2, 1906.</p>
        <p>TORY WORKINGMEN.</p>
        <p>[By J. Howes.]</p>
        <p>The best testimony the Liberal Government can receive is being provided in the torrents of abuse now poured out upon them by the Tory party. Liberal work that would satisfy the Tories would be a com- plete failure from the reformer's standpoint. The people returned this Government to office because the Tories had so grossly mismanaged our national affairs. Therefore, if the Liberals satisfied their opponents it would prove they had betrayed the trust the people reposed in them. If, on the other hand,the Tories are thoroughly disappointsd with everything the Liberal Government does, it will be fairly strong evidence that they are doing right. _ .</p>
        <p>The Tories gensrally spoil their case by an over- dose of denunciation. Their speakers commence in a most tragic vein,bnt they so tear passion to tatters,that their demonstrations generally end more like a scream- ing farce. The (so-called) Tory Workingmen's Con- ference recently held at Warrington is a case in point. The secretary attributed the electoral disaster which had overtaken their party to "torrential abuse and colossal lying" by their opponents. This was a fairly good kick-off for a Tory Conference, but what a poor opinion these Tories must have of the average intelligence of the British elector, to imagine that when we were exposing the doings of the Tory Government we did not know whether we were telling the truth or not. How sad to think that this worthy Tory Government was tamed out of office by a serie4 of misrepresentations! We told the people that the Tory Government had increased our expenditure ; added enormonsly to our' National Debt ; given large sums of money away in doles to the rich ; increased the food taxes of the poor; neglected home legislation; plunged us into a Far which was to have cost 10 millions, but which ultimately cost over two hundred millions; that they promised that the war was to give white men votes, and find a field of labour for our workers ; that after the war people who had votes before were deprived of them, and the labour field was flooded with cheayi Chinese labour. None of these things had happened, these Tory speakers would have us believe. It was all the result of our 4mapications, and the poor elec- tors were so dense that they actually did not know whether these things had happned or not—therefore our misrepresentations prevailed, and the Tory Govern- ment was defeated bv " torrential abuse and colossal lying." Tory work:ingnen are expected to swallow this.</p>
        <p>Mr. Joynson Hicks wrote predicting that there would be another General Election before many Imonths were over. I fancy his reputation as a prophet will be seriously injured by that statement. The chairman, Sir H. Satan-Karr, described the five months' work of the Liberal Government, as a "legis- lative and administrative nightmare," but he did not say who had the nightmare. If it were the Tory party no doubt the description was correct, and I think I may safely predict that during the next year or two, the Tories may have many more horrid dreams. This being a conference of Tory workingmen, the chairman thought it a proper place at which to insult the Labour party, so he spoke of its members as suffering from " swelled heads, and getting a little bit too big for their boots." How pleased the Tory workingmen must have been to hear their fellow- workers slanged in this fashion ; but the Labour party had actually dared to " attack the House of Lords," which the chairman tells us had existed as many centuries as the Labour party had months. If the Rouse of Lords existed before the Labour party, who, I wonder, was it that fed, clothed, and housed these noble -rie e rs, in pre-labour days. Did the peers work themselves, or did the good things of this life fall down upon them from heaven like the manna in the wilderness? Tory workingmen.—How much longer will you allow the class to which you belong, to be insulted and maligned in this manner? Referring to a possible conflict between the people and the Peers, we are told it is a 1,000 to 3 on the Lords. Yes; but only so long as a number of the workers are content to toady to the Tory party. The moment the workers become true to their own interest the odds will be much greater, but on the other side, and then no Tory speaker will dare to abuse the toilers, even at a conference of Tory workingmen. Sir H. Seton-Karr nished his speech by telling his audience that he would spend his "last shilling and his last breath" in endeavouring to turn the present Radical government out of Parliament. When he has spent his last shilling he will not be of much use to the Tory Party, and when be has spent his last breath, he will go where the Radicals cease from troubling and the Tories are at rest. _</p>
        <p>Alderman Bennett advocated what h e calls Tariff Reform, but what the people know as Protection. As long as the Tories cling to this fallacy, and retain Mr. Chamberlain as one of their leaders, the Libra' Party is safe. Alderman Bennett told the conference that one of the causes of their defeat at the polls, was that they had not been quite as keen and energetic in the direction of social reform, in the last few years, as they were some years back. Unfortunate Alderman! What a heresy to preach ! Fancy the Tories getting lukewarm in the cause of social reform. I wonder the conference heard him any more. They had just been told by their secretary, that their disaster was caused by "torrential abuse unrcolossal lying;" then why should this alderman try to unsettle the minds of these innocent Tory workingmen, in, this manner? Why not let them hug the pleasant delusion, that their late Government was made up of all the talents, and that the ingratitude and ignorance of the electorate misled by the" abuse" and "lying" of the Radical party, had secured their defeat? But "Murder will out." At the evening meeting in connection with this con- ference, Mr. R. Pierpoint said "he did not think they ought to get hack to power too soon." I fancy the country is of the same opinion. He also seemed to be very angry with a poster which I was responsible for, at the last election headed " Glorious news about South Africa". 'I am quite prepared to defend evety word on that noster if necessary, but the note in last week's " Examiner " dealt so completely with this matter, that I need not say more about it now. The conference has come and gone. The Tory workingmen have returned to their homes; they will now have leisure to makeup their minds as to whether ' their defeat at the polls was caused by " torrential rbuse and colossal lying," or the slackness of their own 13:Iry in the motter of social reform. They can now help Sir H. Setnn-Karr to spend his last shilling but T hope not to draw his last breath, They can formulate their programme of reforms for the future, , and in the meantime the Liberal Party must keep pegging away at the work which the country returned them to accomplish.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>THOSE EVENING BELLS.</head>
        <p>THOSE EVENING BELLS.</p>
        <p>Those evening bells, how sweet they sound O'er hills and valleys fair ; Conveying to the world around, A solemn call to prayer. Responsive to the tones they love, And never hear in rain., In little groups the weary move To yonder lowly fane. And there, while sunset's glory lends Fresh charms to earth and sky, Their grateful psalm to heaven ascends, And hope lights every eye. For then much nearer, nearer seems Earth's coming Sabbath Day,. Whose life shall crown their brightest dreams, And wipe all ills away. Here they may toil in want and tears, Unloved, unpitied all; And bear, through all thir wears years, The ban of Adam's fall. But 0, sweet thought, unto the poor By mammon long opprest, Love's hand will open wide the door Of everlasting rest. May, IDOL J. H. W. CLINDINNING.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>HORSE GUARDS A DEAD BODY.</head>
        <p>HORSE GUARDS A DEAD BODY.</p>
        <p>A butcher's assistant, Henry Merchant, whilst taking a horse into the stables at Sevenoaks On Wednesday afternoon, fell dead. The animal stood over the corpse for some time until help arrived.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>ELECTION PETITIONS.</head>
        <p>ELECTION PETITIONS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Justice Grantham, on taking his seat on the Bench, informed Mr. Ernest Pollock, K.C., who is one of the counsel in the Bodmin election petition, that it had been definitely arranged to resume the hearing of the Sheffield petition would be commenced on Monday, June n.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>CIENcE NOTES AND NEWS,</head>
        <p>CIENcE NOTES AND NEWS,</p>
        <p>ARTIFICIAL GUTTA-PERGHA. Artificial gutta-percha for telecraPh cables is a mixture of india-ruhber and palm wax, having the same point of fu.ion as iudia-rubber. It rntotts at about C.C.,1..1r. C., and as an electric non- ?onductor it has the same value as gutta-percha. though it is 35 per cent. cheaper.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>LIGHT ACTION ON GLASS.</head>
        <p>LIGHT ACTION ON GLASS.</p>
        <p>Changes in the colour of glass arc caused by subjecting it to the action of ultra-violet rays of light. Something of the same sort may be ob- served on high mountains, where old glass from 'c...ttles, originally green, after exposure to the light of a great elevation, becomes a beautiful pale purple.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>AGATE-CUTTING.</head>
        <p>AGATE-CUTTING.</p>
        <p>The agate-cutting industry is centred at Ober- stein in Germany, where the river Idar in its descent to the Nahe presents a succession of falls through a distance of twenty-four miles, and supplies power for some sixty polishing works. Precious and semi-precious stones are received for polishing from all parts of the world, and the development of the industry has necessitated the use of steam engines and electric motors in place of the water power originally employed. The agates are ground on sandstone wheels; the workman, lying horizontally, presses the agate against the grindstone, obtaining purchase by pressing his feet against a block fixed to the floor. An important branch of the industry is the preparation of blanks for cutting onyx cameos in Paris and Italy. The cameos are cut on stones that have layers of different colours, so that the design appears in white on a red, brown, or black ground.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>GENESIS OF THE PEARL</head>
        <p>THE GENESIS OF THE PEARL</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Paris Academy of Science M. Edmond Perrier, the well-known naturalist, made public an extremely interesting communi- cation which he had received from M. Seurat. This gentleman has been making some very painstaking researches at Tahiti with respect to the genesis of the pearl, and his investigations are likely to alter very" materially the prevalent idea as to how the pearl is formed. M. Seurat states that the germ of the pearl round which the substance is formed composing the pearl is a parasite of the oyster and the giant skate, in the intestines of which it is developed and repro- duced. The parasite is a little tenia of the genus tylocephalum. The fact that pearls are found in fresh water shells goes to shew that another fish in all probability acts the part of the skate in fresh water.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>METEORIC MYSTERY.</head>
        <p>A METEORIC MYSTERY.</p>
        <p>For a quarter of a century museums and col- lectors throughout the world have eagerly sought specimens of the meteoric iron from Canon Diablo, in northern Arizona. This iron has become famous from the number and size of the fragments, from the minute diamonds found in some pieces, and from peculiarities of the locality. It now appears that the crater of Coon Mountain, or Coon Butte, most have been pro- duced by collision with the earth of the very large meteorite, or possibly small asteroid, which these fragments represent. The investi- gations have shown that the formation of the crater and the deposition of the meteoritic mate- rial were simultaneous, that meteoritic material has been found 500 ft. below the surface of the crater's centre, and that sandstone, supposed to be in place, exists at a depth of less than I.oooft. The crater had been previously attributed to vol- canic steam explosion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>IGUANODONS IN BRUSSELS • MUSEUM.</head>
        <p>IGUANODONS IN BRUSSELS • MUSEUM.</p>
        <p>Probably the finest collection of the remains of that remarkable reptile the Iguanodon in the world is that in the Royal Museum of Natural History, Brussels. So great is the number of specimens that a special gallery in the museum has been set apart for their display. The iguanodon, a huge herbivorous reptile, which habitually walled upright on its hind legs, was first found in the Wealden of Tilegate Forest in Sussex by Dr. Mantell—the name Iguanodon be- ing given on account of the resemblance of the teeth to those of the little American lizard, the iguana. The Wealden rocks near :•lons, in Belgium, occupy a remarkable position, and the remains of iguanodon in them were discovered in an in- teresting way. These Wealden strata, in fact, fill up a subterranean valley in the Coal Measures of Hainaut, some 800 ft. in depth, and running east and west. This valley has branch- ing out from it at right angles into the sur- rounding coal measures deep, narrow gorges, also filled up with Wealden strata. In driving a gallery in the colliery of Bernissart, at a depth of 1.056 ft., the miners came to what they would ;call a fault—the coal seams came to an end, and the rocks were replaced by others of a different character, seams of fine sand and clay. It was on,e of these old ravines filled up with the much founger rocks of the Wealden. In these sands and clays the remains of iguanodon were found. In some cases the bones of the skeleton were all in place, shewing that the animals had been entombed entire. These re- mains were carefully collected. and in three years twenty-nine skeletons of iguanodon, besides crocodiles, tortoises, a salamander, and many fishes and plants, were obtained. The bones, being fragile, were covered with plaster as they were laid bare. The study and restoration of these bones was undertaken by Dollo and Bou- langer, and they are now set up in their special gallery in the Brussels Museum. The species was named Iguanodon Bernissartiensis, after the colliery of Bernissart, where it was discovered. It thus commemorates the immense service which ruining may render the science of geology.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>ETHERISED_ PLANTS.</head>
        <p>ETHERISED_ PLANTS.</p>
        <p>- -- At Cornell University, Ithaca, N.Y., extensive experiments in the etherisation of plants have been under way for some time, under the direc- tion of Professor Craig, head of the horticultural department. The objects of the work, says the New York Tribune, are to test the efficiency of ether on the forcing of such herbaceous perennials as aquiie- gias, golden glows, and astilbi japonicas, bulbs of the naroissus, tulip, hyacinth, Easter lily, and of various miscellaneous plants, to determine, if possible, the number of hours plants should be exposed -to ether to obtain best results, to acquire an idea of the amount of ether required, to test the use of ether on forcing of rhubarb and aspa- ragus, to see if comrgon shrubs respond alike to the action of ether, to determine whether it is possible to ripen berries, and thus make saleable for Christmas such plants as the aucubas. and, finally, to obtain an idea of the efficiency of the</p>
        <p>FORCING OF PLANTS BY ETHER from the commercial standpoint. The shrubs are obtained in the fall shortly after the cold weather has set in, and are placed in a room where the temperature is kept, as near freezing as possil)le. When plants are required for use they are te,ltn out a few hours before placing them in ether, in order that they may become comparatively dry before etherisation. The etherising apparatus consist' s of an airtight galvanised iron box. made especially for this pur- pose. 3ft. 3M. by 2ft. 6in. It contains two trays. rendering it possible to have three tiers of bulls or plants 'undergoing tho experiment at onea. The trays are composed of heavy wire mesh, in order that the ether may, permeate every part of the box. In order to render the box absolutely airtight, the cover is so made that it fits down about sin. over the body of the box. resting on a flange or ridge which is overlaid with thick felt. Precautions being taken to clamp the lid on firmly, the box is practically airtight. In plac- ing such plants as aquilegias. golden glows; and spirmas, in the box care must be taken to re- move as much dirt as possible from the roots, giving the ether free access. Shrubs. lilacs, &amp;c., are placed in the box without being potted. When the preliminary work is romnleted. a small ball of felt containing the reavired amount of ether is introduced into the airtight box, and the lid clamped down as rapidly as possible. When remoWd, after twenty-four, thirty-six, or forty-eight hours, as the ease may be. the plants are aired before being 'placed in the forcing- house. Other specimens of the' Ortur plant, known as cheek planta, are put at their side to enable the experimenter to</p>
        <p>MAKE RIS COMPARISONS. The lilac has given the best results, etherised lilacs coming into bloom from eight to ten days earlier than the untreated ones. With Japanese Quince and deutzia the results are not so marked. Under the head of herbaceous plants, golden glows or aquilogists gave little or no results, while on the other hand spirreas testihoides shewed the action of ether in a most remarkable manner, the etherised plant coming inio full bloom from ten days to three weeks before the unetherised. Rhu- barb treated in a like manner was ready, to cut five days before the untreated rhubarb, and yieloied a much larger proportion of edible stocks. In the case of, asparagus. a remarkable pheno- menon was observcd. The plant grew 'riotously. becoming tall and spindly, and was absolutely unfit for use. The stately Easter lily shews indi- cations of blooming from one to three weeks be- fore the unetherised sample. The use of ether on plants marks a revolution in horticulture. The whole subject is still in the experimental stage, but its supporters predict more startling results to come. ine specimens sun3ecrea not only grow with increased rapidity. varying with the particular specimen, but the flower seems to attain a fuller bloom and maturity,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>AFTER FIVE YEARS.</head>
        <p>AFTER FIVE YEARS.</p>
        <p>Time is the best test of truth. Here is a St. Helens story that has stood the test of time. It is a story with a point, which will come straight home to many of us. Over five years after Mr. Leyland, 115, Napier-street, St. Helens, was cured by Doan's backache kidney pills, he said :—" It is all quite true about the good Doan's backache kidney pills did me some years ago, and the best'of it is, they cured me to stay cured, for I have never had my back bad since. I am in splendid health, and have been from the time of my cure. I have recom- mended the pills to a great many people." Mr. Leyland's original statement is as follows :—"On and off for ten years I suffered from kidney disorders. The pains across my back andloins were terrible. It was a trouble for me to stoop, and my work became a burden to me. I had headaches and giddiness, my legs ached, and seemed to give way when [ walked. I felt drowsy and tired, and had no energy. My appetite was poot, but I had an excessive thirst. "Of all the medicines I tried only Doan's backache kidney pills did me good. These pills have cured me. lam free from pain, and my work is no trouble to me now. My health is greatly improved in every way, and I can get about with ease and comfort. I shall gladly tell others how reliable a medicine Doan's pills are. (Signed) Thomas Leyland." Doan's Backache Kidney Pills are two shillings and ninepence per box (six boxes for thirteen shillings and ninepence.) Of all chemists and stores, or post free, direct from Foster-McClellan Co., 8, Wells-street, Oxford-street, London, W. Be sure you get exactly the same kind of pills that Mr. Leyland had.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>PROPOSED CYCLE PARADE.</head>
        <p>THE PROPOSED CYCLE PARADE.</p>
        <p>The townspeople of Warrington will be pleased to hear that it has been definitely decided to hold a fancy dress cycle parade and carnival in aid of the Infirmary extension fund on Saturday, July 21st. The Cycling Club has taken up the project with great enthusiasm and there was a large attendance at a meeting held at the Patten Arms hotel on Friday evening, to elect officers for the parade and transact other preliminary business.— Mr. G. Bowman, captain of the Cycling Club, who was voted to the chair pro. tem., proposed the election of Mr. William Bellian as chairman of the Parade Coniznittea. This was seconded by Mr. E. Pickavance (hon. sec ),who said they had implicit con- fidence in Mr. Bellian. (Applause.) The effort now about to be put forth must be the best the club had yet engaged in, and he hoped that £5OO would be raised as the money was sorely needed. (Applause.) The motion was put to the meeting and carried with much heartiness. Mr. Bellian, on taking the chair, said that in accepting the honour, he felt quite sure he should have their support in bringing to a success- ful termination a cycle parade for the benefit of that grand • institution,), the Warrington Infirmary. (Applause.) When they came to think that their last and deeply regretted chairman, "Pa" Davies, had received such kind attention during his last moments, it ought to be an incentive, if any was needed, for each member of the committee to do all in his power to make the parade to an even more successful one, than any previous effort. It srzmed but a short time since ',they had "Pa " with them, and there was no one would miss his cheerful face more than the committee who had the pleasure to work under him. (Hear, hear). He was afraid he should make a poor substitute for him, but with their kind assistaiice would do his tzst. It was unnecessary for him to gs into any details of the scheme decided on by the Board of lianagement of the Infirmary, and it was also quite apparent the urgency of obtaining more muney. Mr. W. Peter Rylands was appointed Presi- dent of the Parade Committee, and the Mayor (Alderman Bolton), Sir Gilbert Greenall, and Messrs. F. Monks, J. C. Parr, mid w. Long, vice-presidents (with power to add to their number).— Mr. E. Picka- vance was appointed hon. secretary, Mr. A. E. Sanders, assistant secretary, and Mr. Chas. Leigh (hon. treasurer). —Mr. G. Bowman was appointed vice- chairman of the General Committee.--The Finance, Prize and Novelty, and other Committees were appointed.—At the conclusion of the cycle parade it is proposed to hold a smoking concert in the Farr Hall.—At meetings on Monday evening Mr. P. Brad- shaw was appointed chairman and Mr. C. H. Hoult, hon. secretary of the Printing and Advertising Com- mittee ; and Mr. G. H. Smith was elected chairman end Mr. W. R. Baines, hon. secretary of the Novelty Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS. The Cost of Out-relief.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS. The Cost of Out-relief.</p>
        <p>I An interim meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held at the workhouse on Wednesday when there were present :—Mr. J. Billinge (presiding), Mrs. Clay and Mrs. Wilkinson, Dr. Murray, Messrs. S. Davies, A. J. Jolley, G. Thompson, R. Forshaw, T. H. Ashcroft, R. Bolton, A. Brooks J. Cook, G. Mather, W. Flannagan, M. W. Seton' E. L. Bent, J. C. Sutton (clerk), J. F. Awbery (master), and A. Wright (superin- tendent of Padgateschools.) OUT-RELIEF IN NEWTON DISTRICT. Mr. DAVIES, in moving the adoption of the cheques for the fortnight, said he would like to call the atten- tion of the Newton Relief Committee to the fact that during the worst part of the distress the out-relief had risen from £55 to £6O, and then to £75. He was not going to impute carelessness to the Committee, but it did seem strange that now when there were such large orders coming to the works in the district, the amount of out-relief still stood at £75 a fortnight. The CHAIRMAN said that unforturately distress as still with them and the Committee were very careful in fully investigating all cases which came before them. Unfortunately the out-relief was still very high but they anticipated in the near f uture that things would improve and that they would have to submit a smaller sum than that day. Everything that could be done was being done to keep down the out-relief but they were the victims of eircumstancn and where distress existed it had to be relieved. The Viaduct works were now on full time but were employing 500 or GOO fewer hands. The cheques were approved.</p>
        <p>WALKING DAY. The Schools Committee recommended that the usual Walking Day treat be allowed and that the children in the Cottage Homes be permitted to take part in the Padgate and Warrington Walking Day ceremanies. STAFFING OF THE SCHOOLS. The report of the school inspector was considered and it was agreed to further consider on June 2Gth the question of the school staff which was commented Upon. ' The minutes were approve 1. STAFF CHANGES. At a meeting of the House Committee held on May 25th the master reported the death of Miss Barton, chil- dren's attendant, and he was directed to send to the father the condolences of the Guardians. It was resqlved to appoint to the vacancy Miss Kate Willis, now doing temporary duty at an annual salary of .222 10s. together with uniform and the usual residential allowances. FEEDING OF SCHOOL CHILDREN. Mr. BILLINGS said he would present his report on the conference on the feeding of school children, at the next meeting of the Board. Ile CLERK said the Conference was most interesting and the questitn a most important one. Although Socialistic views were expressed the general feeling of the Conferenne was opposed to that.</p>
        <p>1111il</p>
        <p>DEAR CHILDREN,</p>
        <p>I have been reading through all the 'a I have received from you on "Spring,' have at last decided to award the prize of a very nice penknife to Master Ernest Harrold, I think this essay very good, and it was beautifully written nd so tidy that I took all. this into consideration. He is one of our lucky members, as I think this is the second priza sent him within a very short time. Many of you sent me in excellent contributions, but you must not be discouraged at not winning a prize this time; you must all TRY AGAIN. I am also awarding a prize this week to Arthur Frearn' for his interesting story, called Tom Jones' New Year's Party." You will find thii tale printed a little further down these columns. Many of you have sent me good ideas for our next competition, and I think I will decide tO offer two very nice prizes to the boys sending me in by June 26th .the best autobiography of "A Penknife." I think you will all find plenty to say ,about this, and you ought to be able to make up quite an interesting little history. For the girls I propose to also offer two beauti- ful prizes for the best made and prettiest pin- cui.:lion, to be sent in by the same date. This also should, be quite easy for all, as it only requires a little care and trouble. The boys must all remember to write on one side of the paper only, in ink, and very tidily, as all this will be taken into consideration in awarding prizes. If you will all put your ages at the foot of each contribution, I should be glad; I can then make a little allowance for our younger members. I hope you will all do your best in the above competitions, and all try to win prizes.l With all good wishes from UNCLE NED.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—l hope you will excuse me for not sending my prize acknowledgment before, as I have had no time; but I have all day this Saturday, so I liought I would try for the competition on</p>
        <p>rnele Ned,—l am sending you an</p>
        <p>"Sp:4ng." I have been staying in Alfret ,or a few days, and I happened to look "The Children's Corner" in your paper.‘ should like to become a member of yc League. I live at Netherfield, a little villa just outside Nottingham. It is a very prei place, and ws have the railway on both sid of our house, one the Northern and the ot' the Midln- ' look lie'</p>
        <p>our age Aty des</p>
        <p>—her the Midland, so I often go to look at the trains when I have nothing else to do. I should very much like to see my name printed in your paper, so now I must conclude with love and best wishes to you, From your affectionate Niece, EDIS.</p>
        <p>SPRING.</p>
        <p>Spring is the first season of the year. How eagerly we look forward to its advent. Tli,e dull, dark days of winter are past, and the birds begin to sing, and the trees put forth young leaves and blossom. It is a lovely eight, when we walk in the country, to see the hedges decked with green and the beautiful daffodils, crocuses, and the star-like primrose raising their stately heads. It is in Spring that we first hear the bleat of the little lambs. It is my delight to see the wonderful creatures running about. In Spring we look forward to nice picnics, and an occasional row on the river. I like to go on water, and I think 't is simply delightful to be in a boat when the sun is Shining on the water and the birds are pour- ing forth their sweetest songs. All Nature looks so lovely in her new Spring dress. I have been listening for the cuckoo. She comes in Spring, and I shall not be satisfied till I have heard her. I think that Spring is the most pleasant season of the year. God for thee the Spring has given, pour thy soul in gratitude. IDA EDIS.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—l am sending you a story, which I should very much like to see in print and have a prize for, so that I could remember you. It is entitleil TOM JONES' NEW YEAR'S PARTY. Tom Jones had a New Year's party. Now, by itself, that does not seem to amount to much, because parties are very ordinary affairs. But to Tom Jones it was an unheard-of thing. 14 the first place, he was a bachelor; secondly, because he lived in the wilds of America; thirdly, because hie party was the first that had been given in that part of the world. All the men around were invited, and Tom's greatest anxiety was whether the food would last out. He had a grand dinner, in the shape of two pigs, which he had fattened up for the occasion, and he had concocted a big pudding, stuffed full of raisins and other good things, all put together, as nearly as he could remem- ber, in the way .they had made it in the old land during his boyhood. December 30th arrived at length, and brought with it ll its friends. Not one was missing, and many were the jokes as to how they should all shake- down in his old shanty. But Toni had care- fully 'planned it all out, and late that nfs-ht they all stowed themselves away quite happily. It did not seem long before Tom began to drtara that he was killing those pigs. There was a grunt and a squeal, and in an instant Tom found himself sitting upright in bed listsuing intently. All the others were fast asleep, as their heavy breathing testified; but the pigs were not! Another squeal, and Tom v:as at the window. What a eight met his eyes I, There, in the white, brilliant moonliwht, making the place as light as day, lie saw a couple of beans busily eng7;.:-.1 ii trying to, get at his two preciothi Their huge paws were between the la's, and one or twice they had clawed the unfortu- nate piggies. He seized his gun—which he always kept by his bedside ready loaded— aimed, and fired. What a babel arose. Outside</p>
        <p>the bears growled, the pigs squealed; inside he was assailed by a host of questions, non,: of which Tom had time to answer, or he intended to bag those two bears if he could with his own gun. The guests hurried into -their clothes as fast as they could, but before they could get outside both beans lay prone on the white snow, and Tom told thern.that they could have bear steaks as well as pork steaks, if they close. I remain, your loving Nephew. ARTHUR .I:EALtti.</p>
