      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>SPRING.</head>
        <p>SPRING.</p>
        <p>Happy Spring has come at last, and every one, even the birds, are happy. • Spring is the first of the four seasons, begin- ning in March and ending in June. Spring brings many changes of Nature, viz., the days begin to lengthen, and the nights begin to shorten. The time of blossom has also come, and the country, with its trees wrapt in green, has a beautiful appearance. The orchards, with their white apple blossom, look ao though winter had returned; but the cold dull days of 'winter are past, and the white -,snow is replaced by the green grass in the fields and the gloomy, dull weather is gone, and we have instead winds, showers, and sun- shine. Everything Springs into a new life as though by magic, and the birds welcome Spring as we do. Their beautiful songs are heard in the tree-tops, and they build their nests so as to rear their young. The farmer covers his ground with manure, and cultivates it, so as to , get a sufficient crop for the winter, td feed his cattle. The merry housewife is busy with her Spring- cleaning, and the painters and decorators are very active with their work.</p>
        <p>Crici-ei.rePiL'es'ibali,and lawn tennis also becomes one of the favourite sports of Spring. Many people also devote their spare time to cycling._ _</p>
        <p>From your ever loving Nepllew, ERNEST HARROLD.</p>
        <p>HE PITIED THEM.</p>
        <p>A little East End boy was on his first country eicursion. Some birds were flying high over- head, and his hostess, a young woman, said: "Look up, Tommy. See the pretty birds flying through the air." Tommy looked up quickly, and then he said, in a compassionate tone: "Poor little fellers! They ain't got no cages, have they?" Dear Uncle Ned,—l am writing you this small letter, telling you that I should like to be a member of "The Children's Corner," I must close now with best wishes from your loving Niece, _</p>
        <p>N. REDMAN.</p>
        <p>---+--- POSSIBILITIES.</p>
        <p>What does the bulb of the lily know, As it lies, in the cold dank mould, Of itn wavy petals as white as snow, And its wonderful heart of gold'? What does the tiny acorn know, Hid deep in the dark hillside, Of the grandeur that out of its germ may grow,</p>
        <p>The Mighty forest's pride? Sweet as the lily, more fair and bright Than the rose in the desert wild, More grand than the oak in its towering might, Is the soul of a little child! If from blackened roots and shapeless seeds, Such beauty and grandeur spring, What to the spirit that heaven leads Will eternity's mons bring?</p>
        <p>-‘4"vIMMP.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>LIST OF NEW MEMBERS.</head>
        <p>LIST OF NEW MEMBERS.</p>
        <p>Lena Haworth, Arnold Heap, Leah Har- greaves, Mary Jenkinson, Sarah Dyson, Thomas Brown Lizzie Scowcroft, Florence Wilkinson, Isabella Radcliffe, John Whitworth, Mary Ford, Ethel Green, Maggie Riley, Alice Hindle, Walter Holden, Jane Anne Harwood, Margaret Taylor, John Willie Holden' Nancy Alice Carter, Alice Entwistle, Jane Ralph, Ethel Tomlinson, Thomas Rushton, Maria Pilling, and Flossie Garraway.</p>
        <p>ONE WET DAY. Granny told us, one wet day, We might dress in fine array, In the pretty clothes, she says, People wore in olden days. From her oaken chest we drew Coats and gowns of dainty hue; ' And in half-an-hour or so, We were folks of long ago. In our quaint costumes of yore, Off we ran to granny's door, And slowly, hand-in-hand, Danced •a Spanish saraband. Granny thanked us; then she said: "Now, my dears, it's time for tea!' And, hey presto! all the train Turned to modern folks again.</p>
