      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <p>THE CLUBMAIIii The Touring Club Ballot.</p>
        <p>It is announced that 12,726 members of the Cyclists' Touring Club have egercised their right to vote upon the question : "Are you in favour of steps being taken to broaden the basis of the Club,- so as to admit all tourists to membership 7" The result shows that 10,495 are in favour and 2,231 against, and the matter of preparing a scheme, whereby the wishes of the majority may be given effect, has been referred to a special committee. Although the membership of the Club has beep declining of late years, the financial position is very strong, and with an infusion of new blood there is no reason why the institution should not commence a new policy of extended usefulness. There must be many thousands of cyclists in the country to whom the Club's work in secaring good accommodation for its members, at reasonable rates, will appeal if brought under their notice, and with an extended membership it Will be easier for the Club to secure the co-operation of hotel keepers. The possibilities open to the Club are abundantly proved by' the marked success of the Touring Club of France; which has progressed by leaps and bounds on the broader basis about to be adopted in this country.</p>
