      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>Needless Restriction.</head>
        <p>A Needless Restriction.</p>
        <p>A contemporary calls attention to the absurd law which debars unlicensed\refreshment houses from, trving mineral waters after the hour of 10 Rm. Lanier this arrangement, a belated cyclist in need of a ginger-beer must either patronise a. public Locse or bargain for the loan of a glass, so that he may consume his refreshment outside the premises. Although in some districts the law is not observed, it Would seem high time that a restraint upon business and an inconvenience to cyclists and othiv travellers, for which there appears to be no justifi- cation, should be abolished. litdy Cyclists and the Veil.</p>
        <p>Many, lady riders find a difficulty in secnrin their hats in windy weather, and to such the fashion of the motor veil should prove a help. Now Ilia t, the public is accustomed to the appearance of the large gauze veil, enveloping the bat, hair, an.,l fe,';! of the lady motorist, there is nothing consoicuou: or unusual in a small veil or scarf being used to tie the hat, when cycling. A flapping hat is fatal alike to appearance and comfort.</p>
        <p>ition camp of the Association of Cycle Campers, held in the grounds of the Crystal Palace, Which will be continued till Whitson, has so fnt prov,ed a'gre;:t success, the greatest interest being taken by visitors. Some of the members of the ilissocitition, from long practice, are able to erect ,"tb.e.ir tents and place everything in position in • nder the minutes. Before the close ef the :exhibition there is to be held a camp pitching .tompatition. Open to ladies and gentlemen when the North Terrace should present an animated</p>
        <p>=I</p>
        <p>The Lady Who Didn't Know.</p>
        <p>Despite all that has been printed concerning variable gears, there remain some people with the crudest notions respecting their action. A traveller for a famous cycle firm was lately requested by a cycle agent to see a good customer of his, whose wife bad a three-speed bicycle, which she said was too hard to drive. On examining ," the machine the traveller found it fitted with the handlebar change gear, with the lever at the top speed of 86 in. He, then learned that with this gear the lady had been attempting to ride a bill hear, and on questioning her about it she stated that she knew nothing about the lever, but presumed that the three gears (50 in., 65in., and 86 in.) were automatic, and that when she came to a hill the low gear would go on by itself. She was quite surprised to learn that she would have to make use of the lever provided in order to obtain satisfactory results.</p>
