        <p>had done. If they were to believe Mr. Peacock it was Mr. Henshall who introduced and discussed the subject in the railway carriage, while Mr. Peacock had known nothing about it. and had no wish to talk about it until that time. Mr. Swift submitted that the whole of the circumstances of the case corroborated Mr. Henshall's statement. Mr. Henshall could never have known all the details of the committees businesslwhich he had dis- closed if a member of the committee had not told him. Look how strikingly Mr. Henshall was corroborated by the agenda paper incident. Why in the world should he show the agenda to Mr. Heeshall ? He (Mr. Swift) did not see any reason for bringing out the agenda if Mr. Peacock's story was true. Mr. Peacock in his statement at the committee meeting would lead one to believe that lie was viewing the land when he met Mr. Henshall accidentally on the spot, and disclosed his "pet" scheme. The 'pet "scheme factor was a strong one in favour of the prosecution. It was never so much a" pet" as when Mr. Peacock thought he might make 100 for himself out of it. He (Mr. Swift) hadl endeavoured to lay that case before the jury with absolute fairness, and they were now going to hear something not so dispassionate from his friend, Mr. Tobin. After all the jury were trying Mr. Peacock upon the facts, not upon Mr. Henshall's conduct. If Mr. Peacock was guilty, he must not be allowed to get off because Mr. Tobin indignantly demanded to know why Mr. Henshall did not seize him and throw him out of the railway carriage. Mr. Henshall dealt with him as business men did deal with each other, but he had nothing to do with the scheme. If the jury thought Mr. Peacock did lay those proposals and did suggest that he should have a share of the profits it would be their duty—he knew it would be a distasteful duty because nobody got any pleasure out of the prosecution —it would be their painful duty to convict him and he was sure they would not shrink from that duty.</p>
        <p>ALL THE ALLEGATIONS REPUDIATED. The defendant then testified on oath.He stated that in December, 1901, Mr. Hardiman applied to the Borough Treasurer for a part of the Flag-lane land, and the matter came under the notice of the Finance Committee on the 9th January, 1905. Witness wanted firemen's cottages to be built on the whole of the property, and he expressed that opinion at the meeting on the 9th January. He objected to a portion of the block being let to Hardiman, on the ground that it would spoil the sale of the remaining, part of the land, and that it would be the frontage to the best part. The question of the cottages had been a pet scheme of his. It became a pet scheme five or six years ago,and he had always since set his heart on it. He suggested it to the committee two years ago. On the 10th of January witness went to Manchester. He got in at Liverpool, and when the train reached Warrington Mr. Henshall got in. There was another gentleman in the carriage but he could not remember now who he was. Witness came back by the 1.30. He did not return to the station with the intention of catching the 12.30 train. He had his lunch at a restaurant near his works, which would be abbut 20 minutes' walk from the station. His busi- ness would not have permitted him to return by the 12.30 train. Witness took his seat in the train, and Mr. Henshall came and took a seat beside him. As the train moved away Mr. Henshall asked him why he had blocked an application for the lease of land in Flag- lane, and witness showed his agenda paper referring to the matter which he had received on the 6th January. Witness said" Yes I did block it" and Mr. Henshall asked" Why ?" and his reply was because he thought a workshop would spoil the whole property. Witness went on to tell Mr. Henshall his idea in reference to firemen's cottages. They both went to see the land in question and went over it. Mr. Henshall asked him about the remainder of the property, and witness said he was not quite sure whether the whole belonged to the Corporation, but he showed him the piece of land which had been applied for and he (IVIr. Henshall) agreed that a printing works would spoil the whole lot from selling. Mr. flenshall asked witness about his previous ideas with reference) to cottages and suggested that if he was successful in getting the land the plotting out would be a difficulty. He asked witness if he had any objections to contracting for the work. He told Mr. Henshall that, subject to him getting the land, he should have no objection to con- tract for doing it. He had in his mind at the time that the land would be put up by auction and that he should have no objection to supervise as it was work he had done previously for seven years continuously. He said he would not mind contracting to do the work. lie could not swear that he mentioned the sum of £lOO. He should think the work would be worth more than £lOO as there were 1,700 square yards of land, and they might get 16 or 20 cottages on it. Witness had built a considerable number of cottages (about 60), two ware- houses and a chapel. He had always bought his timber from R. A. Naylor's. Mr. Henshall was their traveller at the time. He had come into contact with Mr. Henshall when giving orders for timber. When he (witness) went to the office Mr. Henshall would probably be in and would say" Good-morning." Witness would tell him what he wanted, and he would refer him to one of the managers of the works'. Mr. Tobin : Did you ever make an offer that you would get the thing through if Mr. Henshall gave you £lOO out of the profits? Witness : I never intimated such a thing to him. The only mention of £lOO was as to supervision ? That is so, sir. Further questioned by Mr. Tobin, the defendant said he never saw Mr. Henshall on February 15th. At the meeting on February 6th witness either moved or seconded the resolution that the land be put up for auction. I There would have been no difficulty in finding Mr. Henshall. Heknew his address perfectly well. At the meeting of the Finance Committee he might have 'asked if there had been any inquiry from Mr. Henshall. It would be natural for him to do so after his train interview with him. He did not deny having done so. He remembered seeing the Town Clerk in the committee room at the Town Hall. Witness was probably there on other business, but be might have gone to him speci- ally about Mr. Henshall. He said he had heard it was common talk in restaurants that his name had been coupled with that of Mr. Henshall, and that he was the one who was spoken of at the meeting of the Council lon March 7. Witness's name was not mentioned at the Council meeting ; but he thought it was his duty to go straight to the Town Clerk, and he went. He told Mr. Whittle that the only transaction he could recall was the one with reference to Flag-lane, and what actually transpired with Mr. Henshall was different altogether to what had been described, and that if the statement was repeated he should be obliged to go for Mr. Hensliall, or anyone else, as it was totally untrue. Mr. robin: You do not now suggest there was any malice in connection with the law suit some years ago. Witness : Probably not. I won't suggest anything. Further questioned witness said Mr. Henshall was not a party to the action, but he was present at the trial. He did not suggest Mr. Henshall had invented his story. In answer to Mr. Swift, witness said they did not discuss the sale of the land by the Corporation, because there was no sale then on. They discussed Hardiman's application. It was Mr. Henshall who started the con- versation about the matter in coming back from Man- chester. Witness was in the train when Mr. Henshall came to him. You did not have any conversation going to Man- chester ?—No, except that Mr. Henshall asked what time I was coming back. Did you tell him that you wanted to talk to him about some business ?—Not at all. The interview was sought by him and not by you— Yes. Mr. Henshall left you in a perfectly, friendly way— Yes. Can you tell me why he should suddenly have turned round and suggested that you made an improper sugges- tion to him ?—I don't know. I suggest he has misin- terpreted the whole thing. I dont know why he did it. Could he misinterpret the fact that you said to him : I have a good speculation and I want to talk to you about it. Did you say that? I did not. , He could not he mistaken about that could he ?—He could be. lie could have invented it ?—He could. That was what you mean when you say he has'mis- interpreted it ?-4 clout exactly mean to go as far as that. Will you tell us hew far you mean to go ? Do you say he is mistaken or that he invented it ?—I say he has misinterpreted it. Can you give me any reason why he should invent a story ?—I cannot suggest any reason. In the course of further- cross-examination defendant said he 'was doubtful as to whether he ever received a copy of Mr. Henshall's evidence although he would not deny that he had. Asked again as to whether the County Court action ass the only reason he could suggest for Mr. Henshall's allegation defendant said: "There is only the agitation that has been going on in Warrington. There are always two sides to a ques- tion." Mr. Swift : I understand you igot, a testimonial from the Town Council for your services in taking the purity side ?—Yes. And that if anything Mr. Henshall is supposed to belong to the purity side ?—We are both supposed to belong to the purity side. Mr. Henshallwas a well-known 9,nd recognized member of the agitation. He was seen on the platform at a Non-Trading meeting when Mr. Naylor took the chair about three years ago. Is that any reason why he should tell a false story about you ?—None whatever. Can you suggest any reason ?—I don't know why— I have not the remotest idea. You know Mr. Uenshall never mentioned your name till you had been to the Town Clerk ?—I know he did not mention it to the committee but I cannot say he did not mention it to anyone else, because I had heard it. Continuing defendant said it was heard in a restaurant that Councillor Lord was asked : " Who is this Mr. P. ?" and Councillor Lord said to a friend of witness's that it was Councillor Peacock. Questioned as to his alleged promise to "block "the old proposal, defendant said he did not think he had sufficient influence to block a matter, an rule afi members of the Corporation. That was quited new to EXTREME NERVOUS WEAKNESS him. He did not think he had any influence. And St. Vitus'i Dance Mr. Swift : Then what were yon tituents for ?—To represent them sent by your con- . CURED BY VENDS' SEAWEED TONIC. Mr. Tobin thought that was not a legitimate question Mr. E. JACKSON, 23. Edward-street, off Penny-lane, to put to a man on trial for his liberty. Northwich, writes :—"You will think it very unkind of Defendant, again questioned, said he thought it was me not letting you know about my daughter. We owe right that lie should he discussing with Mr. Henshall a you many thanks for the good you have done her; T can payment to him which must depend upon whether he assure you it has been% great blessing to me and the (Mr. Henshall) was able to make a bargain with the family. She is the joy of our home, as red as a rose Corporation or not, land in the best of health. I can truly say she is entirely Mr. Tobin: From first to last you were anxious to cured of the,extreme nervous weakness and there is now carry out your pet scheme.—Yes. no trace of the St. Vitus' dance ; and all this has been From first to last did you ask anyone to bribe you so accomplished by VEND'S SEAWEED Tome. that you might use your influence-?—I never did. VEND'S SEAWEED TONIC strengthens the body and FINAL ADDRESS FOR THE PROSECUTION. I e , curesck, indigestion, wind, sluggish liver, weak and naenusl ba dizziness, headache, kidney troubles,droPsy Addressing the jury Mr. Swift said the easiest way of and female weakness, and is especially good for chronic meeting an allegation if one were guilty was simply to constipation. Price Is. Vd. and 2s. 9d., at Chemists reverse the positions and that was what the defendant .everywhere.</p>
        <p>• POWERFUL PLEA FOR THE DEFENCE. Mr. Tobin made an eloquent and powerful speech for the defence. He said this was a case upon which he entered with as grave a sense of responsibility as any in which he had ever been engaged, because it was a case of the most supreme importance to Mr. Peacock's life. The jury were trying no ordinary case,they were trying a man under an act of Parliament passed quite recently —in 1889 he thought it was—and a verdict of guilty meant that the Court had power to send Mr. Peacock to prison for two years with hard labour, to inflict, in addition, a fine of £5OO, while further, the Court had power to adjudge him of being incapable of being elected to the Council for seven years from the date of the conviction. It was a supremely grave case, and this charge had been brought against a man whose character would stand him in good stead in the day of peril. There was no suggestion that Mr. Henshall had wickedly invented this story,but he (Mr. Tobin) would endeavour to convince the jury that a mistaken interpretation had been placed by Henshall on a conversation which took place eighteen months ago. There was no suggestion of bad faith against Mr. Henshall Where they had one man against another in a criminal case they were bound to take into account and give due weight to the character that man had borne all his life before his fellow men, and be would like to know if the jury or himself (Mr. Tobin) were on trial,whether they could produce a cleaner re3ord that that of the man who stood before them in the dock that day? He had had, he (Mr. Tobin) supposed, some of the best men in his native town to'speak for him. He had spent nine years in the Council, holding positions of trust on committees, and it was not a common thing for a Town Council to pass a unanimous resolution to three of their members for services rendered to the community. And the resolution was passed in February, 1905, about the time this alleged corrupt conversation took place. But apart from paper testimonials, what greater testimonial could they have than that given by the Town Clerk, who gave evidence with the keenest desire to do justice between the two parties ?—the-Town Clerk who knew the ins-and-outs of the Council and was clever,careful and astute to suspect anything wrong. The Town Clerk said that he knew Mr. Peacock in the Council and out of it, and he was a high-minded straightforward man. He (Mr. Tobin) could demon- strate by reasonable arguments that a conviction in this case would be absolutely unsafe. If there was a reasonable doubt Mr. Peacock was entitled to it, but what he (Mr. Tobin) preferred to depend upon was that Mr. Henshall had, perfectly honestly, placed a wrong construction upon the conversation, and in mercy to Mr. Henshall himself that would be the very kindest view to take,otherwise his conduct became inexplicable. If a corrupt offer had been made to Mr. Henshall what he should have done was to have bade Mr. Peacock good- bye and not have condescended to walk with him to the site of the land and discuss the matter at length. Mr. Henshall was holding himself up in an odious light. Did they not think that when he went to view the land he never thought a corrupt offer had been made. A verdict of "Not guilty" would be no reflection on Mr. Henshall. It would not mean that Mr. Hensha4 had committed perjury or told a lie. It would only antvewrasasttihoen.hapTphieereet mean a mistaken interpretation had been put on the Isan'arla n bestreflection o no n oafnytohnee, nth°case.and Was not Mr. Peacock's conduct that of an honest man? Mr. Henshall had told them at h h • • t e was not intimate ' with Mr. Peacock, and only casually knew him. Were ' they to believe that Mr. Peacock would go to a man he only knew casually and ask for a bribe of £100? It , was absolutely incredible, absolutely impossible to , common sense. If they had been cheek and jowl for , years in and out with speculations, then he could have , understood it. According to the evidence of Mr. Clegg, , Mr. Peacock on the 23rd January informed the Finance Committee that he had been to view the land when Mr. , Renshall was there, and probably Mr. Henshall would Make an offer for it. That was what an innocent man would do, and what a guilty man would have kept dark. On February 6th on the proposition of Mr Clegg, seconded by Mr. Peacock, it was decided that the land should be sold by auction. If Mr. Peacozk had been in hopes of getting £lOO out of it he would not have been a party to the resolution. Then Mr. Henshall actually suggested that on February 15th he met Mr. Peacock in the market, and the latter asked him if he was going to consent to his offer. That was most improbable, because nine days before it was decided to offer the land for 1 sale. Unless they had tho exact words of the allegecFl conversation in the train the jury were not on safe ground, because one was apt to give a wrong impression of a conversation that took place a week or a month ago. He asked the jury with all the earnestness at his command to pause low,' before saying that his guilt had been brought ,aome to Mr. Peacock beyond all doubt— to a man against whom there had never been a breath of suspicion during the whole of Ms career. Mr. Tobin's speenhlasted thirty-five minutes.</p>
        <p>THE GRAVITY OF THE CASE. In summing up the Chairman pointed out the gravity of the case- to a man in the defendant's position and urged the jury to give it their most careful considera- tion. They must also bear in mind that it was a very important case in the public interest. They all took a pride in the honour and integrity of our municipal in- stitutions and it was a source of pride that the mem- bers of the municipalities were, they believed, men of irreproachable honour. If they thought the prisoner had fallen away from that high standard and had been guilty of corrupt practice, he was sure they would not shrink in the public interest from returning a verdict of " Guilty." Sir William briefly reviewed the evidence. An important point was : Why should Mr. Henshall have fabricated a story of this kind? It could hardly have been from malice because if malice had existed between the parties the defendant would not have pro- posed to put Mr. Henshall in fora good thing.</p>
        <p>THE VERDICT. The jury retired to consider their verdict and after an absence of a quarter of an hour returned a verdict of "Guilty," with a recommendation to mercy. Mr. Tobin said there was no need for him to address the Court further. They had defendant's past carer before them and the recommendation of the Court would have its proper weight with the Bench.</p>
        <p>THE SENTENCE. Having consulted in private for a few minutes the magistrates returned to pronounce sentence. Sir William Forwood said : Arthur Peacock, you have been found guilty by the jury of corrupt practices in your position as councillor of the Borough of War- rington. I am sure it is not my wish to say anything to aggravate your feelings,--a man o f your high position —on the present occasion. Of course we view with very great jealousy the honour and integrity of our municipalities, and of those who have to discharge municipal duties, and any deviation from the path of rectitude ought to meet with severe punishment. We, however. take into consideration all that has been said about you and the high character you have hitherto borne, and also the recommendation of the jury to mercy. Our sentence upon you is that you be Rued the sum of £lOO, or in default six months imprisonment, and that you be adjudged incapable of being elected or appointed to any public office for seven years from this date, and you forfeit any such office that you now hold. Mr. Tobin asked for a reasonable timo in which to pay the fine, and Mr. Swift suggested that a month be allowed. The Chairman asked for information as to defeadant's position. Mr. F. It. Roberts said the defendant was a substantial man and he (Mr. Roberts) would not mind going bail for him. The magistrates agreed to release defendant on his own recognisances of £lOO, the fine to be paid within a month.</p>
