      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <p>.</p>
        <p>SPANISH POLITICS.</p>
        <p>[By Charles E. Parker.]</p>
        <p>Saturday in Barcelona was held high holiday. The yellow and red striped colours of Catalonia decorated every post, pillar and person. For three hours we sat and watched a political procession march past, four deep. There were Republicans, Carlists, Socialists, students, workmen's societies, clubs, corporations. deputies &amp;c., &amp;c., —a great popular uprising against the powers and laws made at Madrid. ! It is never easy to estimate the forces which give im- ! petus,driving power to popular movements. Our recent Warrington Parliamentary election will suggest itself. astonishing both sides by its strength of popular feel- ing. If it is difficult at home to trace effect back to cause, it is still more difficult to do so abroad. A stranger's eye finds it hard to get the proper focus so that he can see all in true proportion. Nevertheless, by putting aside the things which are incidental, and groping for the real and permanent, I find in this movement which has taken expression in this demonstration a sign of that which is common to every people who desire to progress, a desire for freedom. To enable us to understand why a whole city, a city almost as big as Live rpool,should be on one side,and that side in opposition to the central power,l must exnlainthat the :province of Catalonia is separated from the rest of Spain by its history, race and language. The modern Catalmians are the issue of a composite ancestry. In their vei us the original Iberian blood mingles with that of the Greeks, Romans, Arabs, Goths, and Gauls, and the product is a race characterized by activity , adventure and business ability, which forms a striking contrast to the slow-moving Castilian and the lazy Andalusian. Geographically, Catalonia is part of Spain. Politically, the people have been obliged to become so by stress of circumstances, but as long as the temperament of the two peoples is so different there will ever be friction between them.</p>
        <p>The political aspirations of the Catalonians are more in the direction of Home Rule, or rather local rule. I do not know how to find a comparison in our own politics to the conditions here. The Catalonians are as dissatisfied with the Central Rule as are our Irish;they are as proud of their country and their separate history as are our Scotch, and they are as active and efficient in manufacturing pursuits as we claim to be in Lancashire. The oratory against centralization now ceases, but at present it takes the form of protest against political offences such as newspaper comment and public speech which may be considered offensive to the Ruling Powers, being tried by a centralized military tribunal. This has been made law by a recent enact- ment. This of course conflicts with a right which we consider to be a central beam in the structure of our British constitution, viz., that a man should be tried by a jury of his peers. This military tribunal is more of the character of a court martial than a trial protected by legal safeguards. The way in which the Imperial taxes are levied gives great dissatisfaction. One method is to claim so much; say ten or twenty per cent., of all money raised for local purposes. A progressive town like , Barcelona has therefore to pay a considerable percent- age of any sum it may raise for municipal purposes. Naturally the less progressive sections of the country are well pleased to have their burdens lightened by the energies of the Catalonians, and the representatives of such obstruct all efforts to change the law. It is easy therefore to understand that political feelings at times run high, and why the Deputies are split into parties representing sections of the country, and do not follow the main line of cleavage which in our own country is represented by the terms Liberal and Con- servative. In regard to representatives of the people in Parlia- ment, nominally there is universal suffrage and yet strange to say the conditions correspond to those of our own country before the passing of the great Reform Act. Seats are bought and sold. If a con- j test does come about the voters are bribed, or what c is simpler the officials conducting the election are ; squared and the votes are co.)ked. This of course : refers to Spain generally and the country districts ~ particularly. There are doubtless exceptions to this '? general rule. What is wanted before all other ! thiuri is that a strong moral force should permeate t!,:is r • people and that the popular will should under it guidance and inspiration speak and act in such a , way that privileged wrong,interwoven as it is with so much of the national affairs,should be exorcised from the body politic. This will take many years. A nation advances towards the light by many devious and and often tortuous ways. But there are signs (on 4 of which is the demonstration of yesterday) that the proper, the common people see the light, it may be afar off, and are turning their faces and their steps, towards it. . i CHARLES E. PARKER. Barcelona, May 20, 1000. 1</p>
        <p>a 7 7?: 1 le ee•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>PHOTOGRAPHIC EXHIBITION. Closing Social.</head>
        <p>THE PHOTOGRAPHIC EXHIBITION. Closing Social.</p>
        <p>The close of the successful exhibition promoted by the Warringtonl Photographic Society was signalised on Friday evening by a social gathering of members and friends in the Lecture Room which had been so tastefully transformed into a Japanese tea room. The president (Mr. F. V. L. Mathias) occupied) the chair and was supported by Mr. James Harding. Mr. Peter Dalton, and Mr. F. G. Taylor (vice-presidents). An admirable programme was provider(' and all the items were thoroughly appreciated. Pianoforte solos were given in creditable style by Mrs. Smith and Mr. Bert ‘Vhitby and violin solos were cleverly executed by Mr. Tom Boothroyd. Mr. Harold Smith effectively sang " The song of the bow" and "The Bandolero "and 2 Mr. Massey gaye excellent Lancashire and Yorkshire- , dialect sketches. During an interval Mr. F. G. TAYLOR moved that .` the best thanks of the Society be tendered to the Museum Committee and to Mr. Iladeley and his staff for the assistance they had so willingly given. He coupled with it the name of Alderman Bennett, chairman of the committee (who had just entered the room), because he knew that anything that tended to the plea.snre and profit of the people of Warrington always had the Alderman's cordial support. Mr. NIATIIIAS seconded and endorsad , that Mr. Taylor had said. Mr. JAMES HARDING, the "father " of the society, supported in a few well chosen words.</p>
        <p>The vote was Very heartily accorded. Alderman BENNETT replied. He said he had no idea when he came to pay a last brief visit to the exhibition that he was going to receive such a kindly welcome and generous hospitality. Although he was not a photographer and perhaps was not able to enjoy the pictures from that point of view, he believed such an exhibition could not fail to be of inestimable valise. If it had done nothing else it had got people to know where the museum was and to pay a visit to that admirable insti- tution. He was quite certain that the amount of work involved in the exhibition must have necessitated the arduous exertions of all connected with the Society from the President end his wife down to the humblest members and their wives and sweethearts. The success of the exhibition must be exceedingly gratifying to all. The following is a list of the prize winners in the Art Union held in conjunction with the exhibition:— £2 25., Mr. E. Summerfteld, Warrington ; £1 is.. Miss Enid Brailsford, Heaton, Yorks ; 10s. 6d., Mr. Thorpe, Penketh, Ir. P. T. Lashford, Warrington, Miss Brooks, Warrington ; 75. M., Mr. H. H. Pearson, Mr. Massey, Mr. 11. Bond, and Mr. F. Brooks (all of War- rington). The prizes took the, form of pictures pur- chased at the exhibition. It is interesting to note that several thousand visitors attended the exhibition during the week. The visitors' book was signed by ladies and gentlemen from various parts of the country.</p>
        <p>Services for Sunday. Bewsev-road Wesleyan Church, preachers, 10.30 Rev, J. manning ' • 6.30, Rev. S. Pitt. Bold-street Wesleyan Church, 10.30 Rev. S. Pitt, 6.30,, Rev. J. Manning. Baptist Church, Golborne-street, 10.30 and 6.30 Rev. H. Dunnico. Evening subject, "The re-union of Christendom hindrances." Baptist Church, Latchford, 10.30 and 6.30, Rev. J. F. Matthews. Morning subject, " Eye witnesses and Ministers " ; Evening subject, " Conquest by the Church." The Sunday school anniversary in connection with the Buckley-street Methodist Free Church will be held on Sunday when the preacher will be Mr. Robert Cameron, M.P. The evening service will be held in the Parr Hall. A musical service will be held in the afterdoon at which the Rev. S. Pitt will deliver an address, and selections will be given by the Apollo Prize Male Choir. Special music will be rendered at each service- Wycliffe Church, 10.30 and 6.30, Rev. John Yongo Morning subject, " A Whitsuntide lesson"; evening subject, "The desirable re-union of Christians—What is, and why we should pray for i</p>
