        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES. Lay Readers' Association. The Bishop of Liverpool held the annual service for the admission of lay readers on Monday evening, at All Saints' Church, Prince's-park. From 40 to 50 lay readers were present, and amongst the new readers, presented by Mr. E. W. Bird, admitted and licensed by the Bishop was William Allen Rooke Goss, St. Paul's, Warrington. At the conclusion of the service the Rev. J. P. and Mrs. Baynes held a reception in the Church Institute. Risley Meth&amp;list Church. Last Sunday, services on behalf of the Sunday School in connection with the above church was held. The preacher for Hie day was Mr. Robert Henshall of Latch- ford. There were good congregations at both the after- noon and evening services, though undoubtedly the wet in the evening prevented some from attending. Anthems were well rendered by the choir. Mr. Ward proving himself to be an efficient leader. The services were much enjoyed and the collection amounted to £7. Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church. At this church last Thursday evening Mr. S. A. Gray of Cliff College, a candidate for the ministry, preached a trial sermon. On Sunday morning, the service was conducted by the Rev. S. Pitt, who preached on the Ascension. Hymns appropriate to the season were sung. In the evening the preacher was the Rev. W. Mellor who took for his text the passages :—" There be many that saw. Who will shew us any good? Lord,lift thou up the light of Thy countenance upon us." (Psalm iy. 6) and" He that sheweth thee, 0 man, what is good; and what doth the Lord require of thee, but to do justly and to love mercy,and to walk humbly with thy God?" (Michah vi. 8). Wyciffe P.S.A. An open meeting of this society was held on Sunday last, when Mr. J. W. Slack presided over a fair attend- ance,andisaid be desired,af ter bearing since he entered the room what they as a P.S.A. had decided to attempt, to congratulate them upon their decision. He referred to the great outlay which was contemplated in the scheme, which; would need at least £9OO, to make the church beautiful, as well as makingfall necessary repairs. He wished the P.S.A. success in their efforts to raise suffi- cient to repair and improve the organ.—Mr. J. Dimkerley, of Ashton-under-Lyne, gave a very practical and inspiring address upon the opportunities the mem- bers of that P.S. A. had in making everything attempted succeed. He took the General Secretary's report read at the annual meeting last Sunday and pointed out where it was possible for 500 members to bring about a greater success in their work. Said he : "Try to keep up the attendance, look well after the absentees, visit the sick, help the needy, and so fulfil the law of Christ." He was pleased to know that they had sprung largely i from his society, as they helped by advice n their early stages. Their society's savings club would pay out to the depositors this year £B,ooo.—The Secretary wel- comed one new member, total now 450, or one more than this time last year. He thanked the members for their vote of sympathy expressed to him in his light affliction. He also asked the ladies present to help their husbands and friends in raising the £lOO needed for the repairs to the organ by taking out cards and so giving a little helping hand of their own. Indepen- dently of their husbands and friends, what a grand day they would have at the opening of the organ if they managed to raise from £lOO to £lso.—Miss Marie Houghton sang "With verdure clad " and " I will extol Thee," both of which deliFhted the assembly.—Mr. W. H. Payton, Mug. Bac., presided at the organ.—The usual collection was taken for the funds of the society. Sunday School Anniversary. The anniversary in connection with the Bold-street Wesleyan Sunday school was celebrated on Sunday when special services were held. Throughout the day these were well attended and were in every way highly successful. The preacher in the morning and evening was the Rev. William Potts of Nottingham, and formerly of Warrington, who delivered appropriate and eloquent addresses. A special feature was the singing of the choir which was very effective in the anthems "Lord God most holy" and" Entreat me not to leave thee" in the morning and "With verdure clad" and "There is a green hill " in the evening. The soloists were Mrs. Booth, Miss Mabel Henshall, Miss E. Taylor, and Mr. A. Weber' (Liverpool Cathedral). A flower service was held in the afternoon. There was an excellent attendance of scholars and an encouraging address was delivered by Mr. J. W. Deakin, Northwioh, Mr. T. Cooper presided. At this service also tke music was of a special character. Miss Carrie Hastings was heard to effect in the solo "Jesus lover of my soul," and Mr. R. H. Pearson's violin solos were highly pleas- ing. Mr. A. Weber, who is an old favourite with War- rington people rendered The Lord worketh wonders" and "The Lord is my light" being heard to great advantage. The flowers which were presented at the services Were afterwards sent in the Workhouse. The collections for the day amounted to £44. PARTIAL PARALYSIS. Three Doctors Failed. CURED BY DR. CASSELL'S TABLETS. THOMAS ANDREWS, 31, Duncombe-street, Wollas- ton Stourbridge, Writes, October 10th, 1904 :—" I have had medicine from three local doctors, but I still ontinted to get weaker,till last Paralysis got into ny legs, and the last stroke had took the use of my right arm and also my lingers; but I am thankful o ray, under your treatment he power has come back to fly arms and fingers. I also offered from Indigestion, ,ut that is better, my eye- ight clearer, and any heed ree from from pain • also uy memory is a great deal ,etter. I recommend your ream nerve weaknesstmneenrttoallsuff and rod paralysis." Mn. ANDREWS. DR. CASSELL'S FLESH- FORMING AND STRENGTHENING TABLETS GUM the most pronounced cases of WRECKED NERVES INDIGESTION BODILY WEAKNESS NERVOUSNESS NEURASTHENIA LOSS OF FLESH AN/EMIA PARALYSIS OF NERVES Abundance of medical proof