        <p>Everybody is not interested in football, but the great majority of people will take a more or less lively interest in the fate which has befallen the famous Manchester City Club. For years, preceding last season, it is clear that the club was managed in defiance of the rules of the governing body. Players were paid illegal and exorbitant sums —one of them , was suspended for attempting to buy a match with ' another club, and generally speaking the employees seem to have been able almost to dictate terms to the employers. Consequently both players and directors have been suspended, while no less than IS of the , former have been fined in cash from 1.25 to 5:100 each. Not only so, but each of the culprits is for- bidden to play again for the City Club. The famous organisation is therefore, at present, about as severely shaken and wrecked as was San Francisco by the earthquake. Probably, a new club will arise from the ashes, but it will be some time before " City " attains its old position of eminence in Association football. The severity of the penalties was merited, and the Football Association is to be congratulated on its determination to keep the game clean and respectable.</p>
